.\" imux(1) manual page. Keep it in step with the code.
.TH IMUX 1 "November 21, 2026" "imux" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
imux \- terminal multiplexer with images in the scrollback
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B imux
.RB [ \-L
.I name
|
.B \-S
.IR path ]
.RI [ command
.RI [ flags ]]
.PP
.B \-L
and
.B \-S
choose the server a command talks to, and starts if it is not running.
A
.I name
of up to 32 of
.BR A\-Za\-z0\-9._\- ,
not starting with a dot, gives the socket
.I $TMPDIR/imux\-UID/name
and the state directory
.IR state/name .
A
.I path
is the socket itself, in a directory that is yours with mode 0700, at most 100 bytes long.
Without a flag,
.B $IMUX_SOCKET_NAME
(a name) is used, else the default server.
A flag wins over the variable, and
.B \-S
over
.BR \-L .
A server started with a flag passes its choice on to its panes, so
.B imux
in a pane talks to that pane's own server.
Two named servers share nothing: not the socket, the log
.RI ( server\-name.log ),
the autosave nor the snapshots.
.PP
Without a command
.B imux
attaches to the server, starting it if needed.
The commands and their flags are described under
.BR COMMANDS ;
those that belong to a feature are described with it, under
.BR WORKTREES ,
.BR PERSISTENCE ,
.BR "AGENT STATUS" ,
.B EVENTS
and
.BR PLUGINS .
.PP
.B imux \-\-version
(also
.B \-V
and
.BR version )
prints
.B imux
and the version number and exits.
It does not contact the server and starts none.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B imux
is a terminal multiplexer: it lets you create, use, and control several terminals from one screen.
A server owns all state and holds sessions; a session holds windows, a window holds panes, and
every pane runs
.B $SHELL
as a login shell on a pseudo-terminal of its own.
Clients attach from a terminal and can detach at any time without disturbing the programs inside.
The server starts on demand and exits when the last pane of the last session exits.
.PP
Inline images (iTerm2, Kitty and Sixel) are part of the cell buffer and the scrollback, not drawn
over them, so they survive scrolling, resizing, and splitting (see
.BR "TERMINAL SUPPORT" ).
The default key bindings are listed under
.BR "KEY BINDINGS" .
.PP
Windows are numbered from
.B base\-index
(0 by default) without gaps: closing one renumbers those after it.
Any number of clients can attach at once; each shows the session it last switched to, and all see
the same live state.
Panes are sized to the smallest client looking at their session; a session nobody looks at keeps
its last size, and one made with
.B "new\-session \-d"
starts at
.B default\-size
(80 by 24 unless set).
A larger client shows the unused area as dim dots, and a client smaller than the window shows its
top left part.
.B window\-size
changes which client decides (see
.BR CONFIGURATION ).
.PP
.B "imux \-\-help"
lists imux's own commands (the session commands, snapshots, worktrees,
.BR agent ,
.BR pane ,
.BR events ,
.BR plugin ,
.BR doctor ,
.BR check\-config )
and the names of the commands the server runs.
\fBimux \fIcommand\fB \-\-help\fR (also \fBimux agent wait \-\-help\fR) shows the flags of one of
imux's own commands.
A command line that does not fit prints the reason and that command's usage and exits 2; a failure
while doing the work exits 1; an unknown command prints the whole usage and exits 2.
A group without a sub-command (\fBimux pane\fR, \fBimux plugin\fR, \fBimux api\fR) prints its help
and exits 2.
.SH KEY BINDINGS
Commands are issued with the prefix key, by default
.BR C\-b ,
followed by a command key.
.B "C\-b C\-b"
sends the prefix to the program in the active pane; a key not listed here is discarded.
.B "set prefix2"
makes a second key start a command like the prefix (off by default; set it to
.B None
to remove it), and
.B "send\-prefix \-2"
types it into the pane the way
.B send\-prefix
types the first.
The prefix and the bindings below are defaults and can be changed in the configuration file (see
.BR CONFIGURATION ).
.PP
Keys are decoded, not matched as bytes, so extended keys
.RB ( "CSI u"
and
.BR modifyOtherKeys )
work too.
.B BTab
is another name for
.BR S\-Tab .
An escape sequence split across reads is held for
.B escape\-time
(10 milliseconds), so that a lone Escape is told apart from the start of a sequence.
Repeatable bindings (the arrow keys, alone and with Control or Alt) can be pressed again without
the prefix within 500 milliseconds; any other key ends the repeat and goes to the pane.
.PP
.PD 0
