.PP
A copy becomes the newest paste buffer, which
.B ]
types (see
.BR "PASTE BUFFERS" ),
and goes to the system clipboard.
.PP
With the mouse, dragging selects, and releasing the button copies and leaves copy mode.
Holding the pointer at the top or bottom row keeps scrolling, faster the further it is outside the
pane.
A double click copies the word and a triple click the line; a double click on blank space copies
nothing and ends copy mode.
The middle button pastes the newest buffer.
.SH PASTE BUFFERS
Each copy becomes a new buffer named
.BR buffer0 ,
.BR buffer1 ,
and so on, newest first; beyond
.B buffer\-limit
(default 50) of these automatic buffers, the oldest are dropped.
Buffers made with
.B set\-buffer \-b
or
.B load\-buffer \-b
take a name of your choosing (at most 64 characters, no control characters), are exempt from the
limit and are kept up to 100.
A buffer holds at most 8 MiB (a longer copy is cut), lives as long as the server, and is not saved
by
.BR save\-session .
.PP
A paste types the text with each line feed turned into a carriage return, as Enter does.
ESC, control characters other than tab, DEL, and the C1 range (U+0080 to U+009F) are dropped, so a
buffer can neither end a bracketed paste early nor send escape sequences; tab and line breaks stay.
.PP
The commands are listed under
.BR COMMANDS ;
without
.B \-b
they work on the newest buffer.
.SH MOUSE SUPPORT
.B imux
always requests mouse events from the outer terminal.
.IP \(bu 2
A click in a pane makes it the active pane.
If the program in the pane has enabled mouse reporting, it also receives the event, with
coordinates relative to the pane.
.IP \(bu 2
Pressing on the border between two panes and dragging moves it with the pointer.
Each side keeps at least one cell.
The border is never reported to the program in a pane.
.IP \(bu 2
A click on a window name in the status line selects that window.
.IP \(bu 2
A click on the session name at the left of the status line displays the session list.
.IP \(bu 2
In a list, a click selects and switches to the entry under the pointer, a click outside the
list closes it, and the wheel moves the selection.
.SS Mouse keys
A key of the root table (\fBbind \-n\fP) may be a mouse event, written as the kind of event, the
button where there is one, and the place:
.PP
.RS
.B MouseDown\fIN\fP\fIplace\fP
.B MouseUp\fIN\fP\fIplace\fP
.B MouseDrag\fIN\fP\fIplace\fP
.B MouseDragEnd\fIN\fP\fIplace\fP
.B DoubleClick\fIN\fP\fIplace\fP
.B TripleClick\fIN\fP\fIplace\fP
.B WheelUp\fIplace\fP
.B WheelDown\fIplace\fP
.RE
.PP
.I N
is the button, 1 (left), 2 (middle) or 3 (right).
.I place
is
.B Pane
(inside a pane),
.B Border
(the line between two panes),
.B Status
(a window's entry in the status line),
.B StatusLeft
(the session name at its left),
.B StatusRight
(the blocks at its right) or
.B StatusDefault
(the rest of it).
The key may have the modifiers
.BR C\- ,
.B M\-
and
.BR S\- :
.B bind \-n C\-MouseDown1Pane ...
.PP
A press is
.BR MouseDown ;
the second and third press in a row on the same cell (within
.BR double\-click\-time )
are
.B DoubleClick
and
.B TripleClick
instead.
A drag belongs to the place where its press was, and
.B MouseDragEnd
is the release after a drag; a release with no drag is
.BR MouseUp ,
in the place where it happens.
.PP
When a mouse key has a binding, its command runs and the event is used up: nothing else sees it,
neither the program in the pane nor imux's own handling.
An event with no binding does what it does without these keys (the list above).
Binding a press does not turn off what that press would have done to the drag or release that
follows it; bind those too to take them over.
So that the command acts on what was pointed at, a press on a pane makes it the active pane and a
press on a window's entry in the status line selects that window, before the command runs; the
other events leave the focus alone.
The built-in behavior cannot be switched off with
.BR unbind ;
a binding replaces it.
.PP
The command sees these variables, in formats, in
.BR run\-shell ,
in
.B display\-message
and in the menu and popup commands:
.BR #{mouse_x} " and " #{mouse_y}
(cells from the top left corner of the pane under the pointer),
.B #{mouse_any_flag}
(1 when the program in that pane asked for the mouse),
.B #{mouse_line}
(the text of the row, no trailing blanks) and
.B #{mouse_word}
(the word under the pointer).
The last two are text a program wrote, and can only go into shell text as \fB#{q:mouse_word}\fP.
All are empty when the pointer is not in a pane.
.PP
.B display\-menu
and
.B display\-popup
opened by a mouse key put the box at the pointer with
.B \-x M \-y M
(centered on the pointer's column, from its row downwards, kept inside the window), or at the window's
entry with
.BR "\-x W \-y W" ;
the variables
.BR popup_mouse_x ,
.BR popup_mouse_y ,
.BR popup_mouse_center_x ,
.BR popup_mouse_center_y ,
.B popup_mouse_top
and
.B popup_mouse_bottom
are the pointer's.
Opened any other way, they mean the center.
.SH COMMANDS
Everything imux does is a command, and a command can be given in four places: at the command
prompt (the
.B :
key), bound to a key (see
.BR bind ),
in the configuration file, and from a shell as
.BI "imux " command\fR.
Commands have a full name and many a short form (\fBneww\fP, \fBsplitw\fP, \fBselectp\fP, ...).
Each is described below with its flags, in groups: clients and sessions, windows and panes, paste
buffers, information and options, and commands for scripts.
.SS Command syntax
The
.B :
key opens a prompt on the last row.
Enter runs the line; Escape or
.B C\-c
cancels.
Words are separated by white space, and single or double quotes group a word with spaces.
An unknown command or a bad argument is reported on the last row for two seconds.
The newest 100 lines are remembered, shared by all clients and lost when the server exits.
Up recalls the previous line and Down the next; Down past the newest restores what was being typed.
Empty lines and repeats of the newest are not remembered.
.PP
From a shell the running server carries the command out and its output is printed.
.B imux
.I command
exits with a failure and prints the reason when the command fails.
Commands that work on a place take
.B \-t
.IR target ;
most that list or create take
.B \-F
.IR format ,
which has the variables of
.B "STATUS LINE"
and these:
.PP
.PD 0
.TP 12
session
.BR #{session_id} ,
.BR #{session_windows} ,
.B #{session_attached}
.TP
window
.BR #{window_id} ,
.BR #{window_width} ,
.BR #{window_height} " (the part of the screen the panes use),"
.B #{window_panes}
.TP
pane
.BR #{pane_id} ,
.BR #{pane_index} ,
.BR #{pane_width} ,
.BR #{pane_height} ,
.BR #{pane_left} ,
.BR #{pane_top} ,
.BR #{pane_right} ,
.BR #{pane_bottom} " (cells from the top left corner of the window, the last two inclusive),"
.BR #{cursor_x} ,
.BR #{cursor_y} " (cells from the top left corner of the pane),"
.BR #{pane_active} ,
.BR #{pane_in_mode} ,
.BR #{pane_input_off} " (1 after " "select\-pane \-d" "),"
.BR #{pane_restore_missing} " (1 while a restored pane is in the home directory because its saved one is gone),"
.BR #{pane_spawned_by} " (the pane whose program asked for this one, as " %N "; empty for a pane you made),"
.BR #{agent_name} " (the name of the agent in the pane, empty without one),"
.BR #{pane_pid} ,
.BR #{pane_dead} " (1 for a pane " remain\-on\-exit " kept after its program ended),"
.BR #{pane_dead_status} " (its exit status),"
.BR #{pane_title} ,
.BR #{pane_current_path} ,
.B #{pane_current_command}
.TP
client
(for
.BR list\-clients )
.BR #{client_name} ,
.BR #{client_readonly} ,
.BR #{client_session} ,
.BR #{client_width} ,
.B #{client_height}
.PD
.PP
In
.BR display\-message ,
a name that is none of these is read as an option: \fB#{status}\fP is
.B on
and \fB#{status\-position}\fP is
.BR bottom .
.PP
A target is
.IB session : window . pane\fR,
each part optional.
A session is its name (exact, else a unique prefix, else a pattern with
.B *
and
.BR ? ),
or
.BI $ id\fR.
A window is its number, its name, a position (\fB!\fP the last one, \fB+\fP and \fB\-\fP the next and
previous, \fB^\fP the first, \fB$\fP the last), or
.BI @ id\fR.
A pane is its number, a position, or
.BI % id
alone, which names the pane wherever it is, or a place in the window:
.BR {left-of} ,
.BR {right-of} ,
.B {up-of}
and
.B {down-of}
(beside the active pane), and
.BR {top} ,
.BR {bottom} ,
.BR {left} ,
.BR {right}
and the corners
.BR {top-left} ,
.BR {top-right} ,
.B {bottom-left}
and
.BR {bottom-right} .
A name may be written
.BI = name
to match exactly, with no prefix or pattern.
.BI agent: name
is the pane of the agent given that name by
.B "agent name"
(for a command that wants a window or a session, that pane's window or session).
It takes the place of the whole target, an agent name never matches a session, window, or pane, and a
name that matches no agent is an error.
Without a target, the pane the command was run in is meant, else the active pane of the session
used last.
.SS Who may run commands
Every command works at the prompt, in a key binding and in the configuration file.
From a shell, most commands (the lists, creating, closing and selecting sessions, windows, and
panes, sending keys, buffers, options) are carried out by the server itself, with no client needed.
Any other command (\fBcopy\-mode\fP, \fBnext\-window\fP, \fBchoose\-tree\fP, ...) is run for a client
looking at the session, and fails when there is none.
.PP
A program inside a pane (its process descends from the pane's shell) can look anywhere: the
list commands,
.BR has\-session ,
.B display\-message
and
.B capture\-pane
of its own pane work.
What it can touch is set by the option
.BR command\-from\-panes ;
the rest fails with
.IR "not allowed from inside a pane" ,
so that a program in a pane, such as an agent talked into something, cannot type into your other
panes by mistake.
The option has three values:
.TP 10
.B off
Look only, as above.
.TP
.B spawned
The default: a program in a pane may act on the panes it spawned, and on nothing else.
It spawns with
.B split\-window
(on any pane of its own subtree, itself included) and
.B new\-window
(in its own session), always as if
.B \-d
were given, so the focus never moves.
It may
.BR send\-keys ,
.BR respawn\-pane ,
.B kill\-pane
and
.B kill\-window
on a pane (a window, whose panes must all be) it spawned, directly or through the panes they
spawned, and
.B capture\-pane
on those and on its own pane.
It may not type into its own pane, nor into one the user made, nor use
.BR \-a .
.TP
.B on
Every command the socket allows, except those that run shell text.
.PP
imux records, from the process that sent the command, which pane made each pane the program in
another pane asked for (\fB#{pane_spawned_by}\fP is its id, empty for a pane you made).
A snapshot keeps these records, by the position of the panes, and a restore gives them back to the
restored panes, which have new numbers: a program that spawned panes can still act on them after
the server was restarted and the sessions restored.
A link that points at a pane that did not come back, at the pane itself, or round a circle is
dropped.
A program in a spawned pane is always held to the panes it spawned itself, whatever the option
says, and to looking while the option is
.BR off :
no pane has more power than the one that made it.
.B spawned\-depth
limits the depth (default 2: a pane you made may spawn a worker, and the worker a helper; the helper
may not spawn), and
.B spawned\-panes\-max
the number of live spawned panes under one pane you made (default 16).
A command over either limit fails with
.IR "limit reached" ,
naming the option.
A
.B split\-window
asked for by a program in a pane is also refused with
.I limit reached
when it would leave either pane under
.B pane\-min\-width
columns or
.B pane\-min\-height
rows; a split you make yourself has no such limit.
.PP
Below
.BR on ,
a program in a pane may never run a command that runs shell text or changes the setup:
.BR run\-shell ,
.BR if\-shell ,
.BR pipe\-pane ,
.BR source\-file ,
the option, binding, hook, and environment commands, menus, selecting a pane or window, resizing
and renaming.
They would outlive the pane or act in your name.
Each such command from a pane that is not refused outright is logged (at the
.B info
level) as
.I pane\-write
with the pane, the command, the target and the result.
A refusal names
.B command\-from\-panes
and, where it applies, the pane that is outside the subtree.
.PP
.B display\-popup
is not part of this ladder: it is allowed from a pane while
.B popup\-from\-panes
is on, with the marks described under
.BR Popups .
A program outside imux, run by you, has none of these limits.
.PP
This is a guard against mistakes, not a boundary against your own account.
A program that detaches itself from its pane (for example by forking and letting its parent exit),
or that attaches a client of its own, looks like you to the server and is not held back.
Requests that are not commands, such as
.BR kill\-server ,
snapshots and worktree operations, are not checked at all.
.SS Clients and sessions
.PD 0
.TP 28
.B imux
Attach to the server, starting it if it is not running.
A new client attaches to the session most recently switched to.
.TP
.BR "imux attach" " [" \-t " \fIname\fP] [" \-drx ]
Attach to the server; fail if it is not running.
With
.B \-t
attach to the session of that name, which must exist.
.B \-d
first detaches every other client of that session (the most recent one without
.BR \-t ).
.B \-r
attaches a client that only watches: what it types and its mouse are dropped, except the keys that
detach it (the prefix and its detach key, or a root binding of
.BR detach\-client ),
and it does not count as the client that last typed for
.BR window\-size\ latest .
It still sets the size of the window like any client (see
.BR window\-size ).
.B list\-clients
marks it
.B (read\-only)
and
.B #{client_readonly}
is 1.
The restriction is made by the client's own request, to guard against slips; it is not a boundary
against other processes of the same user.
.B \-x
is accepted and has no effect.
.TP
.BR "imux new" " [" \-s " \fIname\fP] [" \-AD ]
Create a new session and attach to it, starting the server if it is not running.
Without
.B \-s
the session is named with the lowest unused number; a name already in use is an error.
.B \-A
attaches to the session of that name if it exists (and creates it if it does not);
.B \-D
detaches the other clients of an existing session first.
Any other flag (\fBnew \-d\fR) is the
.B new\-session
command below.
.TP
.B new\-session
.TQ
.BR "imux new\-session" " \-d [" \-s " \fIname\fP] [" \-n " \fIwindow\fP] [" \-c " \fIdir\fP] [" \-x " \fIcols\fP] [" \-y " \fIrows\fP] [" \-P " [" \-F " \fIformat\fP]] [\fIcommand\fP ...]"
At the prompt, create a session and switch to it.
From a shell, create one without attaching, starting the server if none runs; without
.B \-d
it refuses and says how to attach (\fBimux new \-s \fIname\fR).
.B \-n
names its window,
.B \-c
sets the directory,
.B \-P
prints the new place, and a command is run in the first pane instead of the shell (see
.BR new\-window ).
A session made with
.B \-d
is
.B default\-size
(80 by 24) until a client looks at it;
.B \-x
and
.B \-y
give its width and height in cells (10 to 1000; one alone keeps the other from
.BR default\-size ).
.TP
.BR "imux ls" ", " list\-sessions " [" \-F " \fIformat\fP] [" \-f " \fIfilter\fP]"
.TQ
.BR list\-clients " [" \-F " \fIformat\fP] [" \-f " \fIfilter\fP]"
One line per session or client.
A
.B \-f
filter is a format (for example
.BR #{==:#{session_name},work} );
in all four
.B list\-
commands, only the lines for which it expands to something other than nothing or
.B 0
are listed.
.B imux ls
prints each session with its name, its number of windows and whether a client is attached.
It fails if the server is not running.
.TP
.BR has\-session " " \-t " \fIsession\fP"
Succeeds when the session exists.
.TP
.BR kill\-session " [" \-a "] [" \-t " \fIname\fP]"
Close every pane of the current or named session.
Its clients move to another; killing the only session ends the server.
With
.B \-a
every session but that one is closed.
.TP
.B imux kill\-server
Terminate the server and hang up every program in it; the sessions end and are not saved.
It works whatever protocol version the server speaks.
.IP
Every client tells the server which protocol version it speaks before anything else.
The version changes whenever the messages between a client and the server change, so a new build
of imux and a server started by an older one can disagree.
The command or attach then fails with a message that names both versions and the two ways out:
.B imux kill\-server
(the sessions end), or sending
.B SIGTERM
to the server's process number, which is in the message (the sessions are saved first, and
.B restore\-on\-start
brings them back when imux is started again).
A server that predates the check cannot say so; the client reports that it closed the connection
without an answer and gives the same advice.
.TP
.B imux skill [path]
Print the agent skill: a short guide, as a
.B SKILL.md
file, written for a coding agent running in a pane.
It covers how to open a sibling pane without taking the focus, run a command, start and prompt
another agent, read a pane and wait, and what never to do.
The copy is built into this imux, so it matches the commands it has.
.B imux skill path
prints the default place for it, under the home directory.
.B imux setup
installs it there; or install it yourself, with
.B imux skill > FILE
where your agent reads skills from, and run
.B imux doctor
to see whether the file at the default place is there and the same.
.TP
.B imux setup [\-\-man\-dir dir] [\-\-skill\-path file] [\-\-shell name] [\-\-no\-man] [\-\-no\-skill] [\-\-no\-shell]
Install the manpage and the agent skill, and add one line to your shell startup file, unless they are
already there.
The manpage and the skill are built into this imux, so what is installed matches it.
The manpage goes to
.I dir/man1/imux.1
(default
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME/man/man1/imux.1 ,
else
.IR ~/.local/share/man/man1/imux.1 ).
When a manpage came with the binary (a package such as Homebrew's puts it at
.I share/man/man1/imux.1
next to the binary's
.I bin
directory) and nothing is at that default place yet, the manpage is left out and nothing is said about it;
give
.B \-\-man\-dir
to install one anyway. The skill goes to
.I file
(default: the place
.B imux skill path
prints).
The shell line is
.B eval "$(imux init zsh)"
(or
.BR bash ;
for fish,
.BR "imux init fish | source" ),
added once, at the end of the shell's startup file under a comment that names imux:
.I $ZDOTDIR/.zshrc
or
.IR ~/.zshrc ,
.I ~/.bashrc
(or
.I ~/.bash_profile
when that is the only one there), and
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fish/config.fish .
The shell is the one in
.BR $SHELL ,
or the one
.B \-\-shell
names; for any other shell nothing is added and the report says so. A file that already mentions
.B imux init
is not changed, and the rest of the file is kept as it is.
The line makes the
.B tmux
link of a pane come first on
.BR PATH ;
see
.BR "Programs that look for a terminal multiplexer" .
One line is printed for each: installed, added, updated (the file was there but differed, as after an
upgrade), already installed, or left alone (the path is a symlink or not a regular file).
Run it after installing and again after upgrading imux. The install script runs it for you; the Homebrew formula tells you to.
This and
.B imux skill
are the only imux commands that write outside its own state directory, and
.B setup
does so only when you run it.
If
.B man imux
does not find the page, add the
.I man
directory above the one it was written to to
.BR MANPATH .
.TP
.B imux doctor
Print what decides how pictures and the screen are drawn, one
.IB key ": " value
line each, and change nothing: no server is started and no file is written.
It shows:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
the terminal: the environment, its name from XTVERSION, whether it answers the Kitty graphics and
Sixel queries, the size of a cell in pixels, and the picture protocols it accepts;
.IP \(bu 2
the picture mode a client would get with the current options (see
.BR image\-mode ,
.BR image\-prefer ,
.BR image\-probe );
.IP \(bu 2
the configuration file, with every problem in it named by file and line (lines are parsed and
options applied; the files of
.B source\-file
are not followed, and a command line is checked for its syntax only), and the options it sets that
are accepted but have no effect;
.IP \(bu 2
the socket directory and whether a server runs (with its process number), and whether that server
speaks the same protocol version as this imux (a
.B protocol
line; a difference, or a server too old to say, is reported as a
.BR PROBLEM );
.IP \(bu 2
the installed plugins, the four agent glyphs, and whether the agent skill is installed and the same
as this imux's copy (a
.B skill
line).
.RE
.IP
A line starting with
.B hint
says what to change.
The terminal is asked the way an attach asks it, so this needs a terminal; without one the
terminal lines say so and the rest is printed.
.TP
.BR "imux check\-config" " [" \-\-summary "] [" \fIfile\fP ...]
Read configuration files and say what
.B imux
does not take in them, one line per finding as
.IR file:line:\ kind:\ item ,
then the number of findings of each kind and the items found most often.
Without a file it reads the one
.B imux
would read; a file named by
.B source\-file
is read too (to a depth of 8, 64 files).
The kinds that count as problems are
.BR unknown\-command ,
.BR unknown\-flag ,
.BR unknown\-option ,
.B unknown\-variable
and
.B unknown\-modifier
(a name or operator in a format string that
.B imux
does not have),
.B refused
(any other line it rejects) and
.BR unreadable .
The kinds that only inform are
.BR ignored\-command ,
.BR ignored\-option ,
.B user\-option
(\fB@name\fP, a user option; the dry read keeps no value) and
.BR missing\-file .
The exit status is 1 when there is a problem.
Nothing is started, written, or changed; programs run by lines such as
.B run\-shell
and plugins are not run or read.
.B \-\-summary
prints only the counts.
.TP
.BR "imux init" " " zsh | bash | fish
Print the lines that keep the
.B tmux
link first on
.BR PATH ;
see
.BR "Programs that look for a terminal multiplexer" .
.TP
.BR "imux completions" " " zsh | bash | fish | pwsh | nu
Print a completion script for that shell; nothing is started or written.
It offers the commands and the words of the client's own commands (such as
.B imux agent
and
.BR "imux plugin" ),
the option names after
.BR set ,
.BR setw ,
.B show\-options
and their long forms, and the event names after
.BR set\-hook .
Sessions, windows, and panes (the targets) are left to the shell.
Load it with
.B eval "$(imux completions zsh)"
(or
.BR bash )
in the shell's startup file,
.B imux completions fish | source
(or save the output as
.IR ~/.config/fish/completions/imux.fish ),
.B imux completions pwsh | Out\-String | Invoke\-Expression
in the PowerShell profile, or save the output of
.B imux completions nu
to a file and
.B source
it from
.IR config.nu .
For zsh the output can also be saved as
.I _imux
in a directory of
.BR $fpath .
ksh has no programmable completion, so there is no script for it.
.TP
.BR rename\-session " [" \-t " \fIsession\fP] \fIname\fP"
Set the session's name; a name already in use is refused.
.TP
.BR switch\-client " " \-n | \-p | \-l | "\-t \fItarget\fP"
Switch to the next, previous, or last (previously looked at) session, or to the session (and
window) the target names.
.TP
.BR choose\-tree " [" \-s | \-w ]
.TQ
.BR choose\-window ", " choose\-session
The session list, or sessions and windows (default);
.B choose\-session
is the first and
.B choose\-window
the second.
The flags
.BR Z ,
.BR N ,
.BR G ,
.B r
and
.B y
are accepted and have no effect, so
.B \-Zw
works.
.IP
In this list, the repository wizard, the buffer and client lists, and the lists of
.B list\-keys
and
.BR show\-messages ,
the key
.B /
starts a filter: every word typed must appear in an entry's text, in order, and the best matches
come first; a query with a capital letter is case-sensitive.
The query and the number of entries kept are written on the bottom border.
While typing, the arrow keys and Page Up and Down still move, Ctrl-C closes, and Backspace and
Ctrl-U edit.
Enter ends the typing and keeps the filtered list (then
.B /
edits the query again).
Escape clears the query, and Escape on an empty query closes the list.
.TP
.B choose\-client
.TQ
.BR detach\-client " [" \-a "] [" \-s " \fIsession\fP] [" \-t " \fIclient\fP]"
.TQ
.B suspend\-client
.TQ
.B refresh\-client
List the attached clients and detach the one chosen (as
.BR D ),
detach this client (as
.BR d ),
stop it as a job (as
.BR C\-z ),
or redraw its screen.
From a shell (or a key) a client is named as
.B list\-clients
prints it,
.BR client\-\fIN\fP :
.B \-t
detaches that one,
.B \-a
all the others, and
.B \-s
every client of a session.
.PD
.PP
The commands for saving and restoring sessions are described under
.BR PERSISTENCE ,
those for worktrees under
.BR WORKTREES ,
for agents under
.BR "AGENT STATUS" ,
and for events and plugins under
.B EVENTS
and
.BR PLUGINS .
.SS Windows and panes
.PD 0
.TP 28
.BR new\-window " [" \-ad "] [" \-t " \fIsession\fP] [" \-n " \fIname\fP] [" \-c " \fIdir\fP] [" \-e " \fINAME=VALUE\fP] [" \-P " [" \-F " \fIformat\fP]] [\fIcommand\fP ...]"
.TQ
.BR split\-window " [" \-hvdbf "] [" \-t " \fIpane\fP] [" \-c " \fIdir\fP] [" \-e " \fINAME=VALUE\fP] [" \-l " \fIsize\fP] [" \-P " [" \-F " \fIformat\fP]] [\fIcommand\fP ...]"
Create a window or split a pane.
.B new\-window \-a
puts the window right after the current one.
.B new\-window \-t
.I session:N
makes the window only when N is the next number (numbers have no gaps) and refuses one in use.
A command name may be given by its start when exactly one imux command begins with it
.RB ( show\-option
for
.BR show\-options ),
at least three letters; a command alias is looked up first, and a start of several names is an
error that lists them.
.B \-e
(any number, up to 32) adds a variable to the program's environment; a name must be letters, digits
and underscores, a name starting with
.B IMUX_
is refused (imux sets those), and the value is at most 4096 bytes.
.B split\-window
puts the new pane beside the old one with
.B \-h
and below it with
.B \-v
(the default).
Its size is given with
.BI \-l " cells"
or
.BI \-l " percent" %
(also
.BI \-p " percent" ),
cut to what leaves a cell for each half.
.B \-b
puts the new pane before the old one (left of it, or above), and
.B \-l
still sizes the new pane.
.B \-f
splits the whole window instead of one pane: the new pane spans the full width (with
.BR \-v )
or height (with
.BR \-h )
and everything else shares the rest; with
.B \-b
it goes above or to the left.
A command is run by the shell with
.BR \-c ,
and the pane ends with it; several words are quoted for the shell, one word is used as it is.
.B \-d
keeps the selection where it was,
.B \-n
names the window,
.B \-c
sets the directory, and
.B \-P
prints the new place.
.TP
.BR kill\-window ", " kill\-pane " [" \-a "] [" \-t " \fItarget\fP]"
Close the window or pane.
With
.B \-a
.B kill\-window
closes every window of the session but the target, and
.B kill\-pane
every pane of the window but the target.
.BR unlink\-window " [" \-k "] [" \-t " \fIwindow\fP]"
is the same as
.B kill\-window
(a window is never in two sessions, so there is no other link to keep).
.TP
.BR select\-window " [" \-lnp "] [" \-t " \fIwindow\fP]"
.TQ
.BR next\-window ", " previous\-window ", " last\-window
Select a window by number or name, or change window.
.B "next\-window \-a"
and
.B "previous\-window \-a"
go to the next or previous window that has an activity or bell flag, wrapping, and say so when no
other window has one.
.TP
.BR swap\-window " [" \-d "] [" \-s " \fInumber\fP] " \-t " \fInumber\fP"
Exchange two windows (the source defaults to the current one).
The window now at the target position becomes current unless
.BR \-d .
.B swap\-window \-t \-1
(or
.BR +1 )
without
.B \-s
trades places with the window before (after) the current one.
.TP
.BR move\-window " [" \-dakr "] [" \-s " \fInumber\fP] " \-t " \fInumber\fP"
Move a window to the target position; those in between shift by one.
It becomes current unless
.BR \-d .
.B \-a
puts it right after the target window, and
.B \-k
first kills the window that is at the target position.
Numbering has no gaps, so
.B \-r
(renumber) is accepted and does nothing.
.B \-s
and
.B \-t
also take
.IB session : number
(as
.BR "imux move\-window \-s work:1 \-t other:5" ):
a window moves to another session, at that place, or at the end if the number is past the last.
The only window of a session cannot leave it, and
.B swap\-window
works inside one session.
.TP
.BR rename\-window" [" \-t " \fIwindow\fP] \fIname\fP"
Set the window's name; this turns off its automatic naming.
.TP
.BR list\-windows " [" \-a "] [" \-t " \fIsession\fP] [" \-F " \fIformat\fP] [" \-f " \fIfilter\fP]"
.TQ
.BR list\-panes " [" \-as "] [" \-t " \fItarget\fP] [" \-F " \fIformat\fP] [" \-f " \fIfilter\fP]"
One line per window or pane.
.TP
.BR find\-window " [\fItext\fP]"
Find windows of the current session whose name or visible screen holds the text, ignoring case.
One match is selected, several are offered in the window list, none is reported.
Without text the prompt asks.
.TP
.BR select\-pane " [" \-LRUDlmMde "] [" \-T " \fItitle\fP] [" \-t " \fIpane\fP]"
.TQ
.B last\-pane
Select a pane:
.BR \-L ,
.BR \-R ,
.B \-U
and
.B \-D
the neighboring one,
.B \-l
or
.B last\-pane
the one active before.
.B \-m
marks the active pane (unmarks it if marked) and
.B \-M
clears the mark.
There is one mark for the whole server; its window shows the flag
.B M
(see
.BR #{window_flags} ).
.B \-d
stops what a client types (keys, paste, and mouse reports) from reaching the pane, and
.B \-e
lets it through again;
.B send\-keys
is not held back.
.B \-T
sets the pane's title (control characters are dropped, at most 256 characters); a program that sets
its own title replaces it.
.TP
.BR resize\-pane " [" \-t " \fIpane\fP] {" \-L | \-R | \-U | \-D "} [" cells ]
Move the pane's edge, one cell by default.
.TP
.BR resize\-pane " [" \-t " \fIpane\fP] [" \-x " \fIwidth\fP] [" \-y " \fIheight\fP]"
Make the pane that wide or high, as far as the layout allows (a number of cells, or a percentage of
the window such as
.BR 30% ).
From the command line these forms need no attached client.
.TP
.BR resize\-pane " " \-M
Only meaningful as the command of a mouse key (\fBbind \-n MouseDrag1Border resize\-pane \-M\fP).
It stands for what dragging a border does, which imux does by itself, so the command changes
nothing and the drag goes on to the built\-in handling.
.TP
.BR resize\-pane " " \-Z
Zoom the pane to fill the window; the others are hidden, not closed or resized.
Running it again, moving to another pane, resizing, splitting, or closing a pane ends the zoom.
The window shows the flag
.B Z
(see
.BR #{window_flags} ).
.TP
.BR select\-layout " [" \-t " \fIwindow\fP] {" \fIlayout | \-E | \-n }
.TQ
.B next\-layout
Arrange the panes as
.BR even\-horizontal ,
.BR even\-vertical ,
.BR main\-horizontal ,
.BR main\-horizontal\-mirrored ,
.BR main\-vertical ,
.B main\-vertical\-mirrored
or
.BR tiled .
The first pane is the main pane of the
.B main\-
layouts: at most 80 columns or 24 rows and two thirds of the window; the mirrored ones put it on
the right or at the bottom.
.B \-E
keeps the structure and shares the room out in proportion to the number of panes.
.B next\-layout
chooses the layout after the one chosen last (in the order above), the first if none yet;
.B \-n
with
.B \-t
does the same for a named window.
.I layout
may also be a layout string as
.B #{window_layout}
prints it (for example
.BR "a844,80x23,0,0{78x23,0,0,0,1x23,79,0,1}" ).
The panes of the window, in order, take the places the string describes, scaled to the window; a
string with a bad checksum or another number of panes is refused and the layout stays.
.TP
.BR break\-pane " [" \-d "]"
Move the pane to a window of its own and select it; with
.B \-d
the current window stays selected.
.TP
.BR join\-pane ", " move\-pane " [" \-hvdbf "] [" \-l " \fIsize\fP] [" \-s " \fIpane\fP] [" \-t " \fIpane\fP]"
Take a pane out of its window (one left without panes goes) and split it into the window of the
target pane, where it becomes the active pane unless
.B \-d
is given.
.B \-b
puts it before the target (left of it, or above) and
.B \-f
splits the whole window, as for
.BR split\-window .
The window of the source must be another one; the only pane of the only window of a session cannot
be moved.
.TP
.BR swap\-pane " [" \-U | \-D ]
.TQ
.BR swap\-pane " [" \-d "] [" \-s " \fIpane\fP] [" \-t " \fIpane\fP]"
With a direction, exchange the pane with the previous or next one; it stays active.
Without one, exchange the pane with the marked pane of the same window.
With
.B \-s
or
.B \-t
the two panes trade places, in one window or between two windows (also of different sessions).
Each window keeps its focus on the same place, so the pane that moved in is active where the active
pane moved out.
.B \-d
is accepted and changes nothing.
.TP
.BR rotate\-window " [" \-D ]
Move every pane to the previous place (next with
.BR \-D ), wrapping round.
.TP
.BR respawn\-pane ", " respawn\-window " [" \-k "] [" \-c " \fIdir\fP] [" \-t " \fItarget\fP] [\fIcommand\fP ...]"
Start the shell, or the command, again in a pane (for
.BR respawn\-window ,
a window of one pane): the program in it is hung up and the pane keeps its place, size, and number.
.B \-k
is needed unless the program has ended and
.B remain\-on\-exit
kept the pane (a dead pane); the pane then starts again with nothing to hang up.
.TP
.B display\-panes
.TQ
.B clock\-mode
Show the pane numbers (as
.BR q ;
.B display\-panes \-d
.I milliseconds
keeps them up that long, at most a minute), or a clock in the active pane (as
.BR t ).
.B display\-panes
.RB [ \-d " \fIms\fP] \fItemplate\fP"
instead of selecting the chosen pane runs the template for it, with
.B %%
the pane's id (\fB%3\fP).
.PD
.SS Menus
.TP 28
.B display\-menu
.TQ
.B menu
.RB [ \-OM "] [" "\-b lines" "] [" "\-C n|\-" "] [" "\-H style" "] [" "\-s style" "] [" "\-S style" ]
.RB [ "\-t target" "] [" "\-T title" "] [" "\-x position" "] [" "\-y position" "] [\-\-]"
.I name key command ...
.PP
Show a box of items over the panes.
Each item is three words: a name, a key and a command.
An empty name is a rule between groups and stands alone; a name that starts with a dash is shown
dim and never chosen (put
.B \-\-
before the first item when it starts with a dash).
The name, the title and
.B \-t
(the pane the formats are about) are formats, expanded when the menu opens; control characters in
them are removed.
The command is not expanded: it runs as a command line, as typed at the prompt, and expands what
it needs itself.
A rule at the start, at the end or after another rule is dropped, and so is an item whose name is
empty after expansion.
.PP
The key is one character, or
.BI C\- x\fR,
.BR Enter ,
.BR Space ,
.BR Tab ,
.BR Escape ,
.BR Up ,
.BR Down ,
.BR Home ,
.BR End ,
.BR PageUp ,
.BR PageDown .
It is shown as
.RB ( k )
at the right edge when it fits beside the longest name; a key that names nothing a terminal
sends is only shown.
A name too long for the screen is cut and ends in
.BR > .
A menu that does not fit in the pane area is not shown, and a message says so.
.PP
Keys: the item's own key or Enter chooses;
.BR Up ,
.B k
and
.BR Down ,
.B j
move to the previous and the next item that can be chosen, wrapping;
.BR PageUp " (" C\-b ") and " PageDown " (" C\-f ")"
move about five;
.BR Home " and " g ", " End " and " G
go to the first and the last;
.BR q ,
.BR Escape ,
.B C\-c
and
.B C\-g
close it.
An item's key is looked at first, so an item may take
.BR q .
With the mouse, a click on an item chooses it, a click outside the box closes it, the wheel moves
the choice, and the pointer moving over an item makes it the choice.
.TP 8
.B \-b
Line set of the border:
.BR single " (the default), " rounded ,
.BR double ,
.BR heavy ,
.BR simple " (ASCII), " padded " (spaces) or " none .
.TP
.B \-C
The item to start on (counting rules, after the drops above), or
.B \-
for none; by default the first that can be chosen.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-S ", " \-H
Style of the box, of the chosen item and of the border; the defaults are the options
.BR menu\-style ,
.B menu\-selected\-style
and
.BR menu\-border\-style .
.TP
.B \-T
Title, in the top border.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-y
Where the box goes (see
.BR "Position of boxes" ).
.TP
.BR \-O ", " \-M
Accepted so a configuration loads; they have no effect.
.PP
The chosen command runs for the client that looks at the menu, in the place that client is in when
it is chosen.
If it does not parse, a message says so.
.B display\-menu
follows the rules of
.BR "Who may run commands" :
from a program inside a pane it is refused unless
.B command\-from\-panes
is on, and a plugin may never run it.
From a configuration file read at start there is no client to show it to, and it fails.
.SS Popups
.TP 28
.B display\-popup
.TQ
.B popup
.RB [ \-BCEkN "] [" "\-b lines" "] [" "\-d dir" "] [" "\-e VAR=value" "] [" "\-h height" "] [" "\-s style" ]
.RB [ "\-S style" "] [" "\-t target" "] [" "\-T title" "] [" "\-w width" "] [" "\-x position" "] [" "\-y position" "] [\-\-]"
.RI [ "program " ...]
.PP
Show a box over the panes of a window, with a program running in it.
The box takes every key typed in that window, the prefix key included, until it closes; the panes
go on running and drawing underneath.
A window has one popup: asked for another, the command does nothing.
A popup is in no layout: it is not listed by
.BR list\-panes ,
is not saved with the sessions, does not count in the window's pane count, and is closed when its
window goes.
The state of an agent in it is rolled up into the window like that of a pane.
.PP
.I program
is one word, run by the shell of the pane (as
.BR "sh \-c" ),
or several words, quoted for that shell and run the same way; with none, the box runs the shell
itself.
A command given on a line with others does not make them wait; only a command from a shell (see
below) waits.
.TP 8
.B \-b
Line set of the border, as for
.BR display\-menu .
.B \-B
draws no border.
.TP
.B \-d
Working directory of the program; by default that of the active pane.
.TP
.B \-e
A variable for the program, as
.IR NAME=value ;
may be given more than once.
Variables of imux itself (the ones that start with
.BR IMUX_ )
are not taken.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-h
Width and height, with the border: a number of cells, or a number and
.B %
of the part of the screen the panes use.
By default half of each.
A box that would be smaller than three by three cells with a border (one by one without) is not
shown, and one that is larger than the screen is cut down to it.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-y
Where the box goes (see
.BR "Position of boxes" ).
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-S
Style of the box and of its border; the defaults are the options
.B popup\-style
and
.BR popup\-border\-style .
.TP
.B \-T
Title, a format expanded when the popup opens, in the top border.
.TP
.B \-t
The pane the formats and the place are about.
.TP
.B \-C
Close the popup of the window and do nothing else.
.TP
.BR \-E ", " \-k ", " \-N
When it closes (see below).
.PP
When the popup closes:
.TS
l l.
no \fB\-E\fP	T{
the program ends and the box stays, showing what it printed; Escape closes it (a stayed box
drops any other key); while the program runs, Escape and \fBC\-c\fP close the box
T}
\fB\-E\fP	T{
the box closes when the program ends; Escape and \fBC\-c\fP reach the program
T}
\fB\-E \-E\fP	T{
the box closes when the program ends with status 0, and stays when it does not; Escape and
\fBC\-c\fP reach the program
T}
\fB\-k\fP	T{
any key (not only Escape) closes a box that stayed
T}
\fB\-N\fP	T{
forgets the \fB\-E\fP and \fB\-k\fP before it
T}
.TE
.PP
Whatever the program does, pressing the prefix key three times in a row closes the popup.
.PP
The mouse inside the box goes to the program when it asked for the mouse; elsewhere it is
ignored while a popup is open.
A box is kept inside the screen when the screen changes size, and the program's screen is resized
to fit it.
Pictures the program prints are kept and drawn in the box.
.PP
Run from a shell (a command on the socket), the command waits until the popup has closed.
It succeeds when the program ended with status 0 or the box was closed by a key, and fails, saying
the status, when the program ended with another;
.B imux
then returns 1.
The popup is closed when the connection goes away.
.PP
Who may ask for a popup: a key, the configuration, the prompt and a program outside imux.
A process the popup itself started counts as you, since a key or command of yours started it.
A program inside a pane may ask for one while
.B popup\-from\-panes
is on (the default; programs that show a choice in a popup, such as a file finder, need it).
Such a popup shows
.B [from %N]
(the pane) in its top border, drawn by imux and never taken from the program, and the processes
it starts may look but not touch, like those of a pane.
With the option off, a program in a pane is refused unless
.B command\-from\-panes
is on, and the refusal names the option.
A plugin may never run it.
.PP
A program in a pane that may ask for a popup can draw one over any pane, with any text, and read
the keys typed into it, for instance a prompt that looks like another program's.
The marker and the three presses of the prefix key are what show and end it; turn
.B popup\-from\-panes
off when programs in panes are not to be trusted with that.
.SS Position of boxes
.B \-x
and
.B \-y
of
.B display\-menu
and
.B display\-popup
count cells from the top left corner of the part of the screen the panes use (the status line and a
docked panel are not part of it).
.B \-x
is the box's left column;
.B \-y
is the row of the box's
.I bottom
edge, so
.B \-y 10
for a box four rows high fills rows 6 to 9, and a box that would start above the first row starts
on it.
The box is always kept inside that part of the screen.
.PP
Instead of a number:
.TP 8
.B C
The center (the default).
.TP
.B P
.B \-x
the left edge of the pane the box is about;
.B \-y
the box's bottom edge on the pane's bottom edge.
.TP
.B R
.B \-x
only: the box's right edge on the pane's right edge.
.TP
.B L
Where the last box of that kind was.
.TP
.B S
.B \-y
only: against the status line.
.TP
.BR M ", " W
The pointer, and the pointer's column on the status line, when a command bound to a mouse key
opened the box (see
.BR "Mouse keys" ).
Opened any other way there is no pointer, and these mean the center.
.PP
A position may also be a format that uses these variables, all numbers:
.BR popup_center_x ,
.BR popup_center_y ,
.BR popup_width ,
.BR popup_height ,
.BR popup_pane_left ,
.BR popup_pane_right ,
.BR popup_pane_top ,
.BR popup_pane_bottom ,
.BR popup_last_x ,
.BR popup_last_y ,
.BR popup_status_line_y ,
and the
.B popup_mouse_*
and
.B popup_window_status_line_*
ones, which follow the pointer as
.B M
and
.B W
do,
together with the variables of the pane, such as
.BR pane_width .
The old spellings
.B popup_centre_x
and its relatives (\fBcentre\fP for \fBcenter\fP) also work.
.SS Paste buffers
A buffer is text kept by the server; copying in copy mode makes one.
The keys are under
.BR "PASTE BUFFERS" .
.PP
.PD 0
.TP 28
.B list\-buffers
Scrolling list of the paste buffers, newest first, with name, size, and first characters.
.TP
.B choose\-buffer
The same list; Enter pastes the selected buffer into the active pane.
.TP
.BR show\-buffer " [" \-b " \fIname\fP]"
Show a buffer's text in a scrolling list.
.TP
.BR set\-buffer " [" \-a "] [" \-b " \fIname\fP] \fItext\fP"
Make a buffer from the text: a new automatic one, or with
.B \-b
replace the one of that name, or with
.B \-a
extend it.
.TP
.BR delete\-buffer " [" \-b " \fIname\fP]"
Delete a buffer.
.TP
.BR paste\-buffer " [" \-dpr "] [" \-b " \fIname\fP] [" \-s " \fIseparator\fP]"
Type a buffer into the active pane.
A paste is always wrapped in the bracketed paste markers when the program in the pane asked for
them;
.B \-p
is accepted for that and changes nothing.
.B \-d
deletes the buffer afterwards,
.B \-r
keeps line feeds as they are, and
.B \-s
replaces them with the separator instead of a carriage return.
.TP
.BR save\-buffer " [" \-a "] [" \-b " \fIname\fP] \fIpath\fP"
.TQ
.BR load\-buffer " [" \-b " \fIname\fP] \fIpath\fP"
Write a buffer to a file (mode 0600, never through a symbolic link;
.B \-a
appends) or read a file of at most 8 MiB into a buffer.
A path starting with
.B ~/
is in the home directory; a relative one is relative to the directory of the active pane's program.
.TP
.BR capture\-pane " [" \-pJ "] [" \-t " \fIpane\fP] [" \-S " \fIstart\fP] [" \-E " \fIend\fP] [" \-b " \fIbuffer\fP]"
Print a pane's text (with
.BR \-p ),
or put it in a paste buffer.
Line 0 is the first line of the screen, negative numbers go back into the history, and
.B \-
means the very start or end.
.B \-J
joins wrapped lines,
.B \-N
keeps the blanks at the end of each line, and
.B \-e
writes colors and attributes as escape sequences.
.PD
.SS Information and options
.PD 0
.TP 28
.BR show\-options " [" \-qv "] [" \-t " \fIwindow\fP] [\fIoption\fP]"
Print every option with its value (quoted where it needs it), or one option.
.B \-v
prints only the value, and
.B \-q
makes an unknown name silent.
.B \-t
names a window: its own value of a window option, or
.B automatic\-rename
as that window has it, comes before the global one.
At the command prompt it shows the options that nothing has read yet.
.TP
.BR list\-keys " [" \-T " \fItable\fP]"
Print the key bindings, or with
.B \-T
those of one table
.RB ( prefix ,
.BR root ,
.B copy\-mode
or
.BR copy\-mode\-vi ).
At the command prompt it shows every binding in a scrolling list (repeatable ones marked
.BR \-r ), as
.B ?
does; Enter, Escape or
.B q
closes it.
.TP
.BR set\-window\-option " (" setw ") [" \-u "] [" \-t " \fIwindow\fP] \fIoption\fP [" on | off ]
Give one window its own value of a window option: the window of the client, or the one
.B \-t
names.
Without a value the window gets the opposite of what it has now (so
.B bind S setw synchronize\-panes
is a toggle key).
.B \-u
takes the window's value away, and it follows the global option again.
A window without a value of its own follows the global option, which
.B set \-g
and
.B setw \-g
change.
The window options are
.BR synchronize\-panes ,
.B monitor\-activity
and
.BR monitor\-bell .
A window option typed without
.B \-g
in a configuration, where there is no window yet, sets the global option.
Window values are not saved with a session; a restored window starts without any.
.TP
.BR display\-message " [" \-p "] [" \-t " \fIpane\fP] [\fItext\fP]"
Fill in the variables of a pane and show the text on the bottom row of a client looking at the
session (when no client looks at it, nothing is shown, and that is not an error); with
.B \-p
print it instead.
Among the variables are
.BR #S ,
.BR #I ,
.B #W
and
.B #P
(session name, window number, window name, pane number),
.BR #{pane_id} ,
.B #{pane_current_path}
and
.BR #{pane_current_command} .
.TP
.B show\-messages
The last 100 messages of the bottom row, newest first, each with its time.
.TP
.B command\-prompt
Open the command prompt.
.TP
.BR command\-prompt " [" \-1 "] [" \-I " \fItext\fP] [" \-p " \fIlabel\fP] [" \-T " \fItype\fP] [\fItemplate\fP]"
Ask a question on the bottom row.
What is typed replaces
.B %%
in the template, and the result runs as a command line; without a template it runs as typed.
An answer is put in as typed and is not read for
.B %
again.
Escape cancels.
.B \-I
gives the text the answer starts with,
.B \-p
the label (both are formats of the current pane), and
.B \-1
takes one key instead of a line.
A comma list of labels (\fB\-p "from:,to:"\fP, up to nine) asks one question after the other, with
.B \-I
giving a first text to each in the same way (\fB\-I "a,b"\fP).
The answers are
.B %1
to
.B %9
in the template, and
.B %%
is the last one.
Cancelling any of them cancels all.
.B \-T
names the kind of completion and is accepted; none is offered.
.TP
.BR confirm\-before " [" \-p " \fIlabel\fP] \fIcommand\fP"
Ask for a key: the command runs on
.B y
or
.BR Y ,
and any other key cancels.
.TP
.BR set\-environment " [" \-gru "] \fIname\fP [\fIvalue\fP]"
.TQ
.BR show\-environment " [" \-g "] [\fIname\fP]"
Change or list the variables new panes get besides the server's own environment (one set for the
whole server;
.B \-g
is accepted).
.B \-u
keeps a variable out of new panes, and
.B \-r
forgets what was set so the server's value is inherited again.
Running panes are not changed.
.PD
.PP
Options, key bindings and hooks are set with
.BR set ,
.BR bind ,
.BR unbind ,
.B set\-hook
and
.B source\-file
(see
.BR CONFIGURATION ,
.B "KEY BINDINGS"
and
.BR HOOKS ).
A key can run any command the socket accepts, with its flags, for the client that pressed it
(\fBsend\-keys\fP, \fBset\fP, \fBnew\-window \-a \-d \-n\fP, \fBjoin\-pane\fP and so on); its output
is shown on the bottom row, and so is its error.
.BR wait\-for ,
.B wait
and
.B plugin\-reload
cannot be bound.
.SS Commands for scripts
.PD 0
.TP 28
.BR send\-keys " [" \-lXFH "] [" \-N " \fIn\fP] [" \-t " \fIpane\fP] \fIkey\fP ..."
Type into a pane: a word that is a key name (\fBEnter\fP, \fBC\-c\fP, \fBUp\fP, \fBF5\fP) is that
key, any other is text.
.B \-l
types every word as text.
.B \-F
expands each word as a format first (control characters in the result are dropped).
.B \-H
takes the words as bytes in hexadecimal (\fB1b 5b 41\fP, or \fB0x1b\fP) and types exactly those;
if one of them is not a byte, nothing is typed, and nothing is typed either when
.B \-N
is not a number from 1 to 1000.
.B \-X
runs a copy mode action (see
.BR "COPY MODE" )
on a pane in copy mode.
The prefix and key tables are not consulted.
In a window with
.B synchronize\-panes
the keys go to its other running panes too.
.TP
.BR wait\-for " [" \-L | \-S | \-U "] \fIchannel\fP"
Synchronize scripts.
By itself it waits until another
.B "wait\-for \-S"
names the channel (a signal sent before anyone waits is kept for the next wait).
.B \-L
takes a lock on the channel and waits while another holder has it, and
.B \-U
frees it and gives it to the next in line.
A channel name is up to 64 letters, digits,
.BR . ,
.B _
and
.BR \- ;
there are at most 64 channels and 64 waiting clients.
A program in a pane may use it, which is how a command sent to a pane reports back.
.TP
.BR pipe\-pane " [" \-IOo "] [" \-t " \fIpane\fP] [\fIcommand\fP]"
Run a command, under
.BR "sh \-c" ,
that gets everything the pane prints on its standard input
.RB ( \-O ,
the default) and/or whose standard output is typed into the pane
.RB ( \-I ,
looked at twice a second).
With no command the pipe is closed (the command sees the end of its input).
.B \-o
only opens a pipe if none is open.
A command that falls more than 1 MiB behind is stopped with a message, never allowed to slow the
pane down; it is hung up when the pane closes.
The command is not expanded; the pane's number is in
.BR $IMUX_PANE_ID .
It is refused to programs in panes.
.PD
.PP
Two commands run a program for you.
They work in a configuration file, bound to a key and at the command prompt, and nowhere else:
.B imux run\-shell
and the like fail, so neither a program in a pane nor anything that can reach the socket can make
the server run a command.
.PP
.PD 0
.TP 28
.BR run\-shell " [" \-bC "] \fIcommand\fP"
Run
.I command
under
.BR "sh \-c" ,
in the directory of the current pane, in a process group of its own.
When it ends, the first line it printed (standard error included) is shown on the message row, or
.I 'command' returned N
if it printed nothing and failed.
The commands after it wait until it has ended: the rest of the line at the prompt, the rest of a
chain bound to a key (\fBbind x run\-shell a \e; display b\fP), and the following lines of a
configuration file or of a
.B source\-file
(there the server waits for it, and the command must not ask the server for anything).
At a key or the prompt the other clients are not held up.
.B \-b
runs it in the background: nothing waits.
.B run
is
.B run\-shell
in a key binding or at the prompt; in a configuration file it is
.BR "run\-shell \-b" ,
so that a plugin script started there can call back into the server through
.B tmux
(see
.BR "tmux shim" )
while the rest of the file is read.
The command's environment has the
.B tmux
shim first on
.B PATH
and
.B $TMUX
set when
.B tmux\-compat
is on; its commands have the power of the user, not of a pane.
A command still running after
.B run\-shell\-timeout
seconds is stopped with everything it started; at most 8 run at once, and they are stopped when
the server ends.
.B \-C
takes
.I command
as an imux command line instead.
.IP
.I command
is a format.
A variable that a program in the pane could have set (the window and session names,
.BR #{pane_title} ,
.BR #{pane_current_path} ,
.BR #{pane_current_command} )
is refused in it, because it could turn into commands.
Write
.BI #{q: name }
to get the value as one quoted word (with
.B \-C
the text is read as imux commands, so write
.BI #{c: name }
for a double-quoted word instead), or read the environment: every variable is also set as
.BI $IMUX_ NAME
(\fB$IMUX_WINDOW_NAME\fP, ...), together with
.BR $IMUX_PANE_ID " and " $IMUX_SOCKET_PATH .
Numbers, flags, and ids (\fB#{pane_id}\fP, \fB#{window_index}\fP, ...) need no quoting.
The flags
.B \-d
and
.B \-t
of other programs are refused by name.
.TP
.BR if\-shell " [" \-bF "] \fIcondition\fP \fIcommand\fP [\fIelse\fP]"
Run the imux command line
.I command
if the shell command
.I condition
exits with 0, else
.I else
if there is one (the condition is handled like the text of
.BR run\-shell ).
With
.B \-F
.I condition
is a format, true when it expands to something that is neither empty nor
.BR 0 ,
and nothing is run in a shell.
The abbreviation is
.BR if .
Like
.BR run\-shell ,
it holds back the commands after it until the condition has been decided, unless
.B \-b
is given.
.SS Programs that look for a terminal multiplexer
Tools that show a choice in a box (a fuzzy finder with its popup option, the popup mode of a
completion plugin) look for the program
.B tmux
and the variable
.BR $TMUX .
With
.B tmux\-compat
on (the default) every new pane has
.B $TMUX
(the socket, the server's pid and 0, joined by commas),
.B $TMUX_PANE
(\fB%\fP and the pane's number),
.BR $IMUX_SHIM_DIR ,
and that directory first on
.BR PATH .
The directory holds a link named
.B tmux
to the
.B imux
program.
Run under that name, it reads the usual command line of that program (global flags first, flag
values attached to the flag or apart, \fB;\fP between commands) and runs the commands that imux has
on the server of the pane it was started in.
It never starts a server and never reads a configuration, and
.B tmux
alone is refused.
.B tmux \-V
prints
.BR "tmux 3.4" ,
the release whose commands this covers.
.B list\-commands
prints the names imux has (and fails for any other, which is how a program learns a command is
missing).
A command that imux does not have fails with its name; \-C (control mode) is refused.
.B display\-popup
ends with the status of its program, as the program does, so a tool sees its own exit code.
Only panes of this server see the link; the real program elsewhere is untouched.
With
.B tmux\-compat
off, a new pane has none of the variables (any it inherited from where the server was started are
removed) and a nested real program works as always.
Panes that exist when the option changes keep what they have.
.PP
The startup files of a login shell usually rebuild
.B PATH
and put the system and package directories first, which hides the link.
.B imux init
zsh|bash|fish prints a few lines that put it first again, now and before every prompt, and that do
nothing outside a pane.
Add one line at the
.I end
of the shell's startup file:
.B eval "$(imux init zsh)"
(or
.BR bash ),
.B imux init fish | source
.RB "(fish)."
.B imux doctor
run in a pane says whether a plain
.B tmux
is the link and prints that line when it is not.
Programs that do not need a plain name should call
.B imux
itself, which works with or without any of this.
.B run
(the plugin manager's way in) is accepted in a configuration and ignored; use
.BR run\-shell .
.PD
.SH WORKTREES
.PD 0
.TP 28
.BR worktree " [\fB\-\-detach\fP|\fB\-\-branch\fP] [\fB\-\-start\fP \fItext\fP] [\fB\-\-setup\fP \fItext\fP|\fB\-\-no\-setup\fP] [\fB\-\-slug\fP \fItext\fP] [\fIrepo\fP] \fIname\fP [\fIbase\fP]"
.TQ
.BR worktree\-remove " [\fB\-\-force\fP] [\fIrepo\fP] [\fIname\fP]"
Open a window on a new worktree, or remove one; both also exist as
.B imux worktree
and
.B imux worktree\-remove
from a shell.
.PD
.PP
.B imux worktree list
.RI [ repo ]
prints the worktrees of the repository the current directory is in (or
.IR repo ),
one per line with tabs between the columns: the path, the branch
.RB ( (detached)
when there is none), the windows of a running server that show it
.RB ( session:index ,
separated by commas, or
.BR \- ),
and notes
.RB ( main ,
.BR locked ).
It asks git and, if one runs, the server; it opens nothing.
For that reason a worktree cannot be named
.BR list .
.PP
.B worktree
(also
.B imux worktree
from a shell) opens a window on a new git worktree cut from the up\-to\-date remote base branch.
The checkout it runs from is never touched.
.PP
.I name
is the worktree's directory name: a valid git ref name matching
.B worktree\-name\-pattern
entirely.
Unless
.B \-\-detach
(or
.BR "worktree\-detach on" )
is given, a branch named by
.B worktree\-branch\-format
is created and checked out (an existing local branch of that name is checked out as it is).
With detach the worktree stays at the base commit, for when something else, such as a skill, makes
the branch.
.PP
.I repo
is the first word if it is a path (starting with
.BR / ,
.BR ~ ,
.B ./
or
.BR ../ )
or a directory holding
.BR .git ;
otherwise it is the repository of the active pane (for the subcommand, of the current directory).
.I base
is a branch of
.BR origin :
the argument, else
.BR worktree\-base ,
else
.BR origin/HEAD ,
else the first of main, master, develop, trunk.
.PP
imux runs
.B git fetch origin
(on failure a warning, and the last known
.B origin/BASE
is used) and
.B "git worktree add"
on a helper thread, so other panes stay responsive; the answer is a status message, or the
subcommand's output.
An existing worktree of the repository at that directory is reused.
.PP
The window is named
.IR repo : name
and holds the panes of
.BR worktree\-layout :
.B claude
and
.B shell
joined by
.B |
(side by side) and
.B /
(stacked), such as
.BR "claude|shell/shell" .
A
.B claude
pane runs
.B worktree\-command
(empty by default: a plain shell).
.B worktree\-start
(or
.BR \-\-start ),
if not empty, is passed to that program as one quoted word; it needs
.B worktree\-command
and is not passed for a reused worktree.
In it
.BR #{repo} ,
.BR #{name} ,
.BR #{branch} ,
.B #{base}
and
.B #{path}
are replaced.
.PP
.B worktree\-setup
(or
.BR \-\-setup ;
.B \-\-no\-setup
turns it off for one call) is a shell command typed into the
.B claude
pane before the program, for what a checkout needs
.RB ( "set worktree\-setup \(dqnpm ci\(dq" ).
The two are chained, in the syntax of the pane's shell, so that the program starts only if the
setup succeeded:
.B &&
in sh, bash, and zsh,
.B "; and"
in fish, and an
.B if
on the exit status in nushell and PowerShell.
On failure the shell stays at its prompt.
Without
.B worktree\-command
the setup is typed alone.
It runs in the new worktree, and not for a reused one unless
.B \-\-setup
was given.
.PP
The setup is typed as written (it is your own shell text), but in it
.BR #{repo} ,
.BR #{name} ,
.BR #{branch} ,
.B #{base}
and
.B #{path}
are replaced by the value quoted for the shell, never raw (a branch name may hold
.BR $ ,
.B ;
or backticks).
Other
.B #{...}
and control characters are refused with a reason.
The shell is told from its name (see
.B default\-shell
and
.BR shell\-syntax );
for an unknown shell, nothing that needs quoting or chaining is typed, and the answer says so.
The window is saved and restored like any other (see
.BR PERSISTENCE ).
.PP
.B worktree\-remove
(also
.BR "imux worktree\-remove" ;
no key) removes a worktree with
.B "git worktree remove"
and closes its window.
With a
.I name
it is the worktree placed by
.BR worktree\-path ;
without, it is that of the current window (for the subcommand, the one containing the current
directory).
It always refuses the main checkout, a path that is not a worktree of the repository, and a locked
worktree.
Without
.B \-\-force
it also refuses changed or untracked files and commits on no remote
(counted with
.BR "git rev-list HEAD \-\-not \-\-remotes" ,
so detached worktrees and branches without an upstream are covered); the answer says which.
The branch is never deleted, imux itself never deletes a directory (git does), and the last window
of the server stays open.
.PP
In the status line options, these variables describe the window's worktree and are empty for other
windows:
.BR #{worktree_name} ,
.B #{worktree_branch}
(empty when detached),
.B #{worktree_repo}
(the repository's directory name) and
.BR #{worktree_path} .
A window's own tab describes that window, the left and right parts the current one, for example
.BR "set window\-status\-format \(dq #I:#{worktree_name} \(dq" .
They also work in
.BR "imux display\-message \-p" ,
.BR "imux list\-windows \-F" ,
the other formats of a shell command, and the variables of hooks and plugins; there they describe
the window of the target.
Like every value that a repository or a program can influence, they are refused in the text of
.BR run\-shell ,
.B if\-shell
and
.B #(command)
unless written
.BR #{q:name} .
.PP
.B worktree\-branch\-format
is the branch name template, with
.B #{name}
and
.BR #{slug} ;
it must contain
.B #{name}
and give a valid ref name.
.B #{slug}
is the optional description from
.B \-\-slug
(or the second word of the wizard prompt), empty if none; a
.BR \- ,
.B _
or
.B .
left at the end because of that is dropped.
The directory is always the bare
.IR name .
.PP
.RS
.nf
set worktree\-branch\-format "#{name}"                   # ABC\-1  (default)
set worktree\-branch\-format "feature/#{name}"           # feature/ABC\-1
set worktree\-branch\-format "feature/#{name}\-#{slug}"   # feature/ABC\-1\-login\-fix
                                                       # (feature/ABC\-1 without a slug)
set worktree\-detach on                                # no branch; a skill makes it
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B repo\-wizard
(bound to
.BR W )
lists the repositories one level below
.B repos\-dir
(default
.BR ~ ):
visible subdirectories that hold
.BR .git .
Choose with the arrow keys or
.B j
and
.BR k ,
Enter, or a click;
.B q
or Escape cancels.
A prompt asks for the worktree name and optionally a slug, separated by a space, and runs
.B worktree
there with the base from
.BR worktree\-base .
.SH PERSISTENCE
.B imux
keeps the layout of its sessions across a server restart, a reboot or a crash, without a plugin.
Saved, per session: its windows and their names, the splits of each window with their sizes, and
which window and pane were active; per pane: its working directory and the foreground program.
Scrollback, images, and program state are not saved.
.PP
The server writes an autosave every
.B autosave\-interval
seconds (default 300, 0 turns it off) while something has changed, and once more on SIGTERM.
The next server restores it when its first client attaches
.RB ( restore\-on\-start ,
on by default).
Sessions return with fresh shells in the saved directories, and
.B imux new \-s
.I name
joins a restored session of that name instead of failing.
A directory that is gone becomes the home directory: a message names it once, the autosave keeps the
saved path until the pane leaves the home directory, and
.B #{pane_restore_missing}
is 1 meanwhile.
The autosave is removed when the server ends on purpose
.RB ( "imux kill\-server" ,
or the last pane of the last session closing) and stays after SIGTERM, SIGHUP, a crash or a reboot.
.PP
A pane that ran a program starts it again only if its name is in
.BR restore\-commands :
the saved command line is typed into the new shell, each word quoted in that shell's syntax (see
.BR shell\-syntax ;
for a shell imux cannot quote for, nothing is typed), and a program with control characters in its
arguments is never started.
Other programs leave a plain shell.
Only a listed program has its arguments written to the snapshot; for any other the file holds the
name alone, because command lines often carry secrets
.RB ( "mysql \-pSECRET" ,
tokens in headers).
.PP
A program that runs inside another (a shell started in the pane, then
.B vim
in it, or
.BR "nix develop" )
is saved with the programs around it, up to four, outermost first.
Restore types them in order, each as its own line.
A plain shell
.RB ( bash ,
.BR zsh ,
.BR fish ,
without arguments) needs no entry in
.B restore\-commands
and is typed as its bare name, so the file cannot choose which file runs; anything else must be
listed.
If one of them may not be started, none is, and the pane gets a plain shell.
A pane whose program was started by an agent is saved as an agent pane.
.PP
A pane that ran the agent program
.RB ( claude )
is resumed with
.B claude \-\-continue
when
.B restore\-claude
is on; that continues the most recent conversation of the directory, so two such panes in one
directory resume the same one.
The command is typed as soon as the shell starts, which relies on the shell keeping typed\-ahead
input while it initializes.
PowerShell throws it away, so there it is typed once the pane has been quiet for a moment (at most
10 seconds).
A PowerShell pane has no job control, so its program is the single child of the shell.
.PP
A program can say exactly how to come back:
.B "imux pane report\-agent ... \-\- command"
(see
.BR "AGENT STATUS" ).
The command is saved with the pane and, if it passes the checks again on load (a plain command
name in
.BR restore\-commands ,
at most 64 arguments, no apostrophes or control characters), is typed instead of the guesses above.
With a command, an agent resumes the pane's own conversation, not just the newest of the directory.
A hook or wrapper of the agent can report it; imux never installs one.
For example, run from a session start hook:
.PP
.RS
.nf
[ -n "$IMUX_PANE_ID" ] && imux pane report-agent --source my-hook --agent my-agent --state idle -- my-agent --resume ID
.fi
.RE
.PP
Persistence saves only the foreground program of a pane and the programs around it, and only their
command lines as the system reports them: a program that rewrites its own arguments is restored
with those.
Pictures and scrollback are not saved.
.PP
Named snapshots are kept until deleted, also when the server ends.
.B C\-s
and
.B C\-r
after the prefix run
.B save\-session resurrect
and
.B restore\-session resurrect
and show the answer in the status line; each save replaces the last, and a session that is already
running is left alone when it is restored, unless nobody has used it (a new server's empty
session gives way to the saved one of the same name).
Other keys, other names or none at all are a matter for
.B bind
and
.B unbind
in the configuration file.
The
.B imux
subcommands print the server's answer and need a running server.
.TP
.BR save\-session " [\fIname\fP], " "imux save" " [\fIname\fP]"
Write a named snapshot, or the autosave when no name is given.
Names use letters, digits,
.BR . ,
.B _
and
.BR \- ,
at most 64, not starting with a dot.
.TP
.BR restore\-session " [\fIname\fP], " "imux restore" " [\fIname\fP]"
Add the sessions of a named snapshot, or of the autosave, to the running server.
A session whose name is in use is left alone and counted in the reply, unless the running one is
untouched (one window with one pane, a shell in front, nothing typed into it by a person or a
script and no agent, which is what a new server starts with; what the shell printed by itself does
not count): the saved session takes its place, and the clients in it move to the saved one.
A client must have attached once, since the pane size comes from it.
.TP
.BR list\-snapshots ", " "imux snapshots"
List the named snapshots.
.TP
.B "imux snapshots \-\-history"
List the rolling copies of the autosave, newest first, as
.IR history/NAME ,
with their age and the sessions they hold.
It reads the state directory and needs no server.
Every
.B autosave\-history\-interval
seconds, when the autosave is written and its layout differs from the newest copy, a copy goes to
.I snapshots/history/
(mode 0600) and the oldest beyond
.B autosave\-history
go.
They survive
.B kill\-server
and are never restored by themselves:
.B "imux restore history/NAME"
adds the sessions of one to the running server.
.TP
.BR delete\-snapshot " \fIname\fP, " "imux delete\-snapshot" " \fIname\fP"
Remove a named snapshot.
.SH AGENT STATUS
Programs that report with
.B "OSC 7501"
(an agent does so once the pane answers its query) show up in the status line and in the session and
window pickers.
A pane is
.BR working ,
.B blocked
(waiting for the user; the agent says why, for example a permission prompt),
.B done
(finished a piece of work), in
.BR error ,
or idle.
A window shows the most urgent state of its panes, and a session or the whole server likewise, in
the order blocked, error, working, done, idle.
.B done
and
.B error
count per client until it has had the pane in front of it (the active pane of the window it looks
at); then the pane is idle for that client.
A
.B working
under 150 milliseconds is shown as the state before it, because some agents send one between a
denied permission or an Esc and its final
.BR idle .
.PP
There is no fixed place for the symbols; put these format variables where you want them, for
example in
.B status\-right
and
.BR window\-status\-format :
.TP
.B #{window_agent_glyph}
the glyph of the window being drawn (nothing when idle)
.TP
.B #{window_agent_state}
its state as a word, empty without a report
.TP
.B #{agent_glyph}
the glyph of the most urgent state anywhere
.TP
.B #{agent_state}
that state as a word
.TP
.B #{agent_count}
how many panes are in it, empty when one or none
.PP
The glyphs are the options
.BR agent\-glyph\-working ,
.BR agent\-glyph\-blocked ,
.B agent\-glyph\-done
and
.B agent\-glyph\-error
(Nerd Font hourglass, circle\-slash, check\-circle and circle\-slash; set plain text such as
.B !
if the font lacks them), drawn in
.BR agent\-style\-working ,
.BR agent\-style\-blocked ,
.B agent\-style\-done
or
.BR agent\-style\-error .
After a glyph the style returns to the status line's own, not to what an earlier
.B #[...]
had set.
A click on a glyph in the right part of the status line opens the agent panel.
.PP
.RS
.nf
set status\-right "#{agent_glyph}#{agent_count} %H:%M"
set window\-status\-format " #I#{window_agent_glyph} #W "
set agent\-glyph\-blocked "!"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Programs that do not speak
.B "OSC 7501"
can report over the socket.
Every pane's environment has
.B IMUX_ENV
(1),
.B IMUX_PANE_ID
and
.BR IMUX_SOCKET_PATH .
.TP
.B "imux pane report\-agent [pane] \-\-source s \-\-agent a \-\-state st"
.TQ
.B "[\-\-seq n] [\-\-message text] [\-\- resume command]"
Set the pane's record: state
.BR idle ,
.BR working ,
.BR done ,
.B blocked
or
.BR error ,
the agent's name (shown as the application) and a message.
The pane defaults to
.BR $IMUX_PANE_ID .
.B \-\-source
names the integration (letters, digits and
.BR :._\- ,
at most 80; not
.B osc
or starting with
.BR imux: ).
.B \-\-seq
must rise with every report of a source (a time stamp does well and is the default); a report not
higher than the last of that source is acknowledged and ignored, so late reports cannot undo newer
ones.
It only works from a process running inside the pane it names (checked through the process at the
other end of the socket), else it is refused.
.TP
.B "\-\- command"
At the end of a report, names the command that brings the program back after a restart.
It must start with a plain command name listed in
.BR restore\-commands ,
have at most 64 arguments and 8 KiB, and none may hold an apostrophe or a control character.
It lives as long as the program: it is dropped when the pane's foreground process is the shell
again.
.TP
.B "imux pane release\-agent [pane] \-\-source s"
Clear the record, if that source wrote it.
.TP
.B "imux agent list [\-\-json]"
One line per pane with a record: pane number, session and window, state, name, and message.
.TP
.B "imux agent get [pane | \-\-current] [\-\-json]"
All fields of one pane's record: the state, who reported it, the program, its kind and progress
when blocked or working, the message, how long ago the state changed, whether a client has looked
at the pane since
.RB ( seen ),
the highest accepted
.B \-\-seq
of each source, and the resume command.
A pane without a record says whether nothing has reported on it or its source released it.
Without a pane, a program in a pane means its own pane
.RB ( $IMUX_PANE_ID ,
which
.B \-\-current
also says).
.TP
.B "imux agent wait [pane | \-\-current] [\-\-until states] [\-\-timeout seconds] [\-\-json]"
Wait until the pane's agent is in one of the states (comma separated; default anything but
.BR working ),
print it and exit.
It returns at once if the agent already is; it fails if the agent exits, another one takes the pane,
the pane closes, or the timeout (seconds, default none) passes.
.TP
.B "imux agent prompt pane [\-\-wait] [\-\-timeout seconds] [\-\-json] [\-\-] text..."
Type the text into the pane of an agent and press Enter.
It is typed as a paste (inside the paste markers when the program there asked for them, line
breaks as Enter, no escape or other control character), at most 8 KiB; the text
.B \-
is read from the standard input.
Off unless
.B agent\-control
is on.
Refused when the pane has no agent record, is dead, or the agent is
.B blocked
(the text would be taken as the answer to its question).
A program in a pane may only do it when
.B command\-from\-panes
is on too, or is
.B spawned
(the default) and the pane is one it spawned.
Flags go before the text: after it, everything is text (so is
.BR \-\-json ).
Keys other than text are sent with
.BR "imux send-keys" .
.IP
Without
.B \-\-wait
it prints
.B sent
and exits.
With it, it waits until the agent has gone through a turn: it returns the first state the agent
reaches after the text was typed that is not
.B working
(like
.BR "agent wait" ),
or fails at the timeout (seconds, default none, counted from the typing, so it covers both) or
when the agent exits or another program takes the pane.
A settled state counts only once the agent was seen
.B working
or
.B blocked
after the text (or was working when the text came);
.B blocked
and
.B error
answer at once, and an
.B idle
or
.B done
that began after the text without that does not.
An agent that shows neither within
.B agent\-stall\-time
seconds (5) of the text fails the call with
.IR prompt_stalled .
A stall or a timeout does not show that the text was not delivered: read the pane
.RB ( "agent read" )
before typing it again.
A settled state is not the end of a task: an agent that was already working ends the wait with the
turn that was running.
Use it on an agent that is idle or done; typed into a working one, the text is queued by the
program.
.TP
.B "imux agent name pane (name | \-\-clear) [\-\-json]"
Give the agent in a pane a name, or with
.B \-\-clear
take it off.
A name is a lowercase letter, then lowercase letters, digits,
.B _
and
.BR \- ,
at most 32.
It is unique among the panes that have a record, and belongs to the record: it is gone when the
program releases or ends it, and when another program takes the pane; a new state of the same
program keeps it.
Wherever a pane is taken by
.BR "agent get" ,
.BR wait ,
.BR prompt ,
.B read
and
.BR name ,
a name works too, and so does
.BI agent: name
as the target of any command
.RB ( "send\-keys \-t agent:reviewer" ).
A name that matches nothing is
.IR not_found .
The name shows in
.BR "agent list" ,
.BR "agent get" ,
the agent panel,
.B #{agent_name}
and the event
.BR agent\-state\-changed ,
and a snapshot keeps it: a restored pane gets it back with the first record its program reports
(if no other agent has it by then).
A program in a pane may name the agent in its own pane and in a pane it spawned (see
.BR "Who may run commands" ).
Underneath it is the socket command
.BR "api\-agent\-name pane name" ,
which a plugin may run with
.BR windows ;
.B "api\-agent\-resolve name"
(look only) turns a name into a pane number.
.TP
.B "imux agent start name [\-\-kind kind] [\-\-pane pane] [\-\-timeout ms] [\-\-json] [\-\- arg...]"
Start an agent program in a pane that is at its shell prompt, give it
.IR name ,
and wait until it reports.
Needs
.B agent\-control
on.
The command typed is never the caller's: without
.B \-\-kind
it is the option
.B worktree\-command
(an error while that is empty), and with one it is the program the option
.B agent\-kind
lists for that kind, which must also be in
.BR restore\-commands .
The words after
.B \-\-
are added as arguments, each quoted for the pane's shell (at most 64, 8 KiB, no control
characters; not for a shell imux cannot quote for).
The pane is given with
.B \-\-pane
(a number,
.BI % id
or any target; without it, the active pane for you at the keyboard, required from inside a pane) and
must have a shell in the foreground, no agent record, and be alive, else the answer is
.IR not_ready ;
the pane that asks is never the target.
.IP
Ready is the first report of state
.B idle
(or
.BR done )
from the program, by OSC 7501 or
.BR "imux pane report-agent" ;
imux does not read the screen.
The wait is
.B \-\-timeout
milliseconds (3000 to 300000, default 30000).
A program that reports
.B blocked
ends the wait at once with
.I not_ready
and keeps its name; one that reports nothing in time ends it with
.IR timeout ,
and the name is not kept.
A client that goes away ends only the wait: the program keeps running and its first report
gives it the name.
The name stays with the program that was started: if it drops its record or reports as another
program while it starts up (behind a dialog, say), the name goes to its next record, until the
shell is back at its prompt or the name is changed with
.BR "agent name" .
It prints
.B "ready %id"
and with
.B \-\-json
.BR {"pane","name","state"} .
A program in a pane may only start an agent in a pane it spawned (see
.BR "Who may run commands" ).
Underneath it is the socket command
.BR "api\-agent\-start name kind pane timeout_ms [arg...]" ,
and a plugin needs
.BR windows .
Flags go before the
.BR \-\- .
.TP
.B "imux agent read pane [\-\-lines n] [\-\-ansi] [\-\-json]"
Print the recent text of a pane: its last
.I n
lines (1 to 10000, default 100), history included, wrapped lines joined, the empty rows that end the
screen left out.
The pane needs no agent record.
The text is plain; with
.B \-\-ansi
the colors and attributes stay as escape sequences.
A program in a pane may read its own pane and, at
.B spawned
or
.BR on ,
the panes it spawned (underneath it is
.BR capture\-pane ,
with the same rules).
.TP
.B "imux pane run pane [\-\-json] [\-\-] text..."
Type a line into any pane and press Enter, as
.B "agent prompt"
does, with no agent needed: the text is typed as a paste (inside the paste markers when the program
there asked for them, line breaks as Enter, no escape or other control character), at most 8 KiB;
the text
.B \-
is read from the standard input.
It prints
.B sent
once the bytes are written, which does not mean the program has run them.
Calls are written in the order they are made.
A pane that has an agent record is typed into only when
.B agent\-control
is on, and not while the agent is
.BR blocked ;
a dead pane is refused.
A program in a pane may only do it, like
.BR send\-keys ,
in a pane it spawned (see
.BR "Who may run commands" ).
Underneath it is the socket command
.BR "api\-pane\-run pane text" ,
and a plugin needs
.BR send\-keys .
Flags go before the text.
.TP
.B "imux pane wait\-output pane (\-\-match text | \-\-regex re) [\-\-source s] [\-\-lines n] [\-\-timeout ms] [\-\-json]"
Wait until the pane shows a line that holds the text, or that the regular expression matches, and
print that line (the first such line).
What the pane shows now is looked at first, so a line that is already there ends the wait at once.
.B \-\-source
is
.B recent
(the default: the screen and
.B \-\-lines
lines of history above it, 100 by default, up to 10000) or
.B visible
(the screen only).
Wrapped rows are joined, colors and attributes are left out, and a regular expression is matched
against one line at a time (the syntax of
.BR regex\-lite :
no backreferences or look-around; at most 512 bytes, as is the text).
.B \-\-timeout
is in milliseconds; without it the wait lasts until the line comes, the pane closes
.RI ( not_found ),
its program has ended in a pane kept by
.B remain\-on\-exit
.RI ( not_ready ),
or the caller goes away (a wait whose client left is dropped at once).
The server holds at most 64 waits, and 8 for one caller (a pane, or you and everything outside a
pane); over that the command fails with
.IR "limit reached" .
Nothing polls: a wait looks at its pane again only when the pane printed something.
With
.B \-\-json
the answer is
.B {"pane":"%3","line":"...","text":"..."}
with the text that was searched.
A program in a pane may wait on its own pane and, at
.B spawned
or
.BR on ,
on the panes it spawned.
Underneath it is the socket command
.B "api\-pane\-wait"
(it never runs from a key), and a plugin needs
.BR read\-panes .
.SS JSON output
.B \-\-json
on
.BR "agent list" ,
.BR "agent get" ,
.BR "agent wait" ,
.BR "agent prompt" ,
.BR "agent read" ,
.B "pane run"
and
.B "pane wait\-output"
prints one JSON value on the standard output instead of the text: an array of records for
.BR list ,
a record for
.BR get ,
.B {"pane":"%3","state":"idle"}
for
.BR wait ,
.B {"pane":"%3","sent":true}
for
.B prompt
and
.B pane run
(with
.B \-\-wait
also
.BR state ),
.B {"pane":"%3","lines":100,"text":"..."}
for
.BR read ,
and
.B {"pane":"%3","line":"...","text":"..."}
for
.BR wait\-output .
A record has the names the events use (\fBsession\fR, \fBwindow\fR, \fBpane\fR, \fBstate\fR,
\fBmessage\fR), ids as strings
.RB ( $1 ,
.BR @2 ,
.BR %3 ),
and leaves out what it does not have
(\fBagent\fR is the program, \fBname\fR the name given with \fBagent name\fR).
.PP
An error with
.B \-\-json
is
.B {"error":{"code":"...","message":"..."}}
on the standard error with exit status 1; the standard output stays empty.
Without
.B \-\-json
the message is the same text, with no code.
Words that are wrong exit with status 2 in every mode, with the reason and the usage on the standard
output.
The codes are
.BR not_found ,
.BR ambiguous_target ,
.BR invalid_argument ,
.B permission_denied
(the message names the option that allows it),
.B limit_reached
(it names the option that limits),
.BR timeout ,
.BR agent_blocked ,
.BR agent_not_running ,
.BR prompt_stalled ,
.BR invalid_regex ,
.BR not_ready ,
.B server_unavailable
(no server runs) and
.B protocol_mismatch
(see
.BR "imux doctor" ).
.B "imux api schema"
prints these codes and the fields above as one line of JSON, for a tool that wants to check what
it reads.
.PP
The two server commands behind the JSON of
.B list
and
.BR get ,
.B api\-agent\-list
and
.B "api\-agent\-get pane"
(run as words, like any command), look only: a program in a pane may run them at every
.BR command\-from\-panes ;
a plugin needs
.B read\-panes
for the second.
.SH AGENT PANEL
.B agent\-panel
(bound to
.BR A )
lists every pane with a status report: glyph, name, message, and time since the state changed.
An agent given a name with
.B "agent name"
shows it in place of the window's.
A window made by
.B worktree
is named by its ticket; windows with several agent panes get
.BR .1 ,
.BR .2 ...,
and with several sessions the session name goes in front.
The message is what the program sent (for example,
.IR "approve Bash: touch x" )
or else a plain word.
The last lines of the selected pane show below the list (beside it in the full view on a wide
terminal).
.PP
Keys:
.B j
and
.BR k ,
the arrow keys, Page Up and Down,
.B g
and
.B G
move; Enter jumps to the pane (its session, window, and focus);
.B q
or Escape closes.
.B /
filters the rows by the name and message of each (the same keys as in the pickers; the query is
shown after the title of the full view and of a docked panel on the side).
The wheel moves the selection; a click outside the box closes it.
With
.B agent\-panel\-jump
.B click
one click on a row jumps; with
.B doubleclick
a click only selects (and previews), and a second click on the row, or Enter, jumps.
Without any reports a message says so.
.PP
With
.B "agent\-panel dock"
the list stays on one edge for every window and session and the panes make room for it (they are
resized when it opens and closes; it is not shown if the panes would get under 20 columns or 5 rows).
.B A
shows it and gives it the keyboard, with the same keys except that Escape and
.B q
only hand the keyboard back, and Enter jumps and does so too.
.B A
in the focused dock hides it; while it is shown but unfocused,
.B A
focuses it.
A click on a row jumps (with
.BR doubleclick ,
it focuses the dock on that row).
The dock shows no preview.
.PP
Options:
.TP
.B agent\-panel
.B overlay
(a box over the panes, default),
.B full
(the whole screen) or
.B dock
.TP
.B agent\-dock\-position
.TQ
.B agent\-dock\-width
.TQ
.B agent\-dock\-height
edge
.RB ( left ,
.BR right ,
.B top
or
.BR bottom ;
default right) and thickness: columns for left and right (32), rows for top and bottom (8)
.TP
.B agent\-overlay\-width
.TQ
.B agent\-overlay\-height
size of the overlay in percent of the screen (70 and 60)
.TP
.B agent\-panel\-preview
.TQ
.B agent\-panel\-preview\-lines
whether to show the selected pane's last lines, and how many rows (8)
.TP
.B agent\-panel\-sort
.B state
(blocked first, then error, working, done, idle; newest first within a state; default),
.B recent
or
.B order
(session, window, and pane order)
.SH HOOKS
A hook is an imux command line that runs when something happens.
.TP
.BR set\-hook " [" \-agpRuw "] \fIname\fP[\fIslot\fP] [\fIcommand\fP]"
Set what runs for the event
.IR name .
.I command
is a command line like those of a key binding (several commands are separated with
.BR \e; ),
often
.BR run\-shell .
A hook is a list of at most 16 commands, numbered from 0, run in order.
Without a slot,
.B set\-hook
replaces the whole list with
.IR command ;
.B \-a
adds it at the end; with a slot (\fBset\-hook pane\-exited[2] ...\fP) only that one is set.
.B \-u
removes the hook or, with a slot, that one command.
.B \-R
runs the hook now, without event variables.
The scope flags
.BR \-g ,
.B \-w
and
.B \-p
are accepted and mean the same thing: imux has one scope.
At most 64 hooks can be set, each command at most 4096 bytes.
A name must be lowercase letters, digits and
.BR \- .
A name imux does not know is kept and reported once in the log, so that a configuration written for
another program loads.
.TP
.BR show\-hooks " [" \-g ]
List the hooks, one line per command:
.IR name [ slot "] " command .
It works at the prompt and from a shell.
.PP
A hook may be set in a configuration file, bound to a key, typed at the prompt or given from a shell
(\fBimux set\-hook ...\fP); the last way is subject to
.B command\-from\-panes
like every command that changes the setup.
.SS Events
Every command has an event
.BI after\- command
(\fBafter\-new\-window\fP, \fBafter\-split\-window\fP, \fBafter\-set\-option\fP,
\fBafter\-bind\-key\fP, ...), which fires once the command has finished, whether it came from a key,
the prompt or a shell.
The words of the command are in
.BR #{hook_argument_0} ,
.B #{hook_argument_1}
and so on (at most ten) and its name in
.BR #{hook_command} .
.PP
The other events are:
.TP
.BR session\-created ", " session\-closed ", " session\-renamed ", " session\-window\-changed
.TQ
.BR window\-linked ", " window\-unlinked ", " window\-renamed ", " window\-layout\-changed ", " window\-pane\-changed
.TQ
.BR pane\-exited ", " pane\-title\-changed ", " pane\-mode\-changed
.TQ
.BR client\-attached ", " client\-detached ", " client\-resized ", " client\-session\-changed
These fire when the state shows the change at the end of a pass of the server, so one event can
stand for several changes made together.
A window is linked when it is created and unlinked when it closes (imux does not link a window into
several sessions).
.B pane\-mode\-changed
is copy mode starting or ending.
.TP
.B pane\-died
The program of a pane ended and
.B remain\-on\-exit
kept the pane.
.B #{hook_exit_status}
is its exit status (128 and the number of the signal if a signal ended it).
A pane that goes away instead fires
.BR pane\-exited .
.TP
.BR client\-focus\-in ", " client\-focus\-out
The terminal window of a client gained or lost the keyboard focus (needs
.BR focus\-events ).
.TP
.BR pane\-focus\-in ", " pane\-focus\-out
A pane became or stopped being the active pane of the current window of its session (needs
.BR focus\-events ).
Changing the window counts.
.TP
.B command\-error
A command failed.
.B #{hook_message}
is the text the user was told.
.TP
.B agent\-state\-changed
The agent in a pane changed state or message.
.B #{hook_state}
is
.BR idle ", " working ", " blocked ", " done " or " error ,
or empty when the agent is gone, and
.B #{hook_name}
is the name given with
.B "agent name"
(the event also fires when the name changes; the event stream has it as
.BR name ).
.TP
.BR worktree\-created ", " worktree\-removed
A worktree was opened or removed.
.B #{hook_path}
and
.B #{hook_branch}
tell which.
.TP
.BR alert\-activity ", " alert\-bell ", " alert\-silence
A window that nobody views got an activity, a bell or a silence flag (see
.BR monitor\-activity ,
.B monitor\-bell
and
.BR monitor\-silence ).
Fires once when the flag appears, not again while it stays.
.B #{hook_pane}
is the pane that caused it.
.PP
These names are accepted but nothing fires them yet:
.BR pane\-set\-clipboard ,
.B client\-light\-theme
and
.BR client\-dark\-theme .
.SS Variables
Inside a hook command these formats are set, as far as the event has them:
.BR #{hook} " (the event name),"
.BR #{hook_client} ,
.BR #{hook_session} " (" $N ),
.BR #{hook_session_name} ,
.BR #{hook_window} " (" @N ),
.BR #{hook_window_name} ,
.BR #{hook_pane} " (" %N ),
.BR #{hook_state} ,
.BR #{hook_exit_status} ,
.BR #{hook_message} ,
.BR #{hook_path} ,
.BR #{hook_branch} ,
.B #{hook_command}
and
.BR #{hook_argument_0} ...
Control characters are removed from the values and they are cut to 256 characters.
.PP
Names, titles, and messages can be set by a program in a pane.
Like the other such formats, they are refused in the text of
.BR run\-shell " and " if\-shell
(see above): write
.BI #{q: name }
or read the environment, where every variable is set as
.B $IMUX_HOOK_WINDOW_NAME
and so on.
Ids,
.B #{hook_state}
and
.B #{hook_exit_status}
need no quoting.
A hook command is read again when the hook runs, so a
.B $NAME
in double quotes is a configuration variable and is replaced then: write the environment
variable in single quotes and escape its dollar sign:
.br
\fBset\-hook window\-renamed "run\-shell 'echo \e$IMUX_HOOK_WINDOW_NAME'"\fP
.SS Limits
A hook started by a hook may start a third one and no more; the rest is dropped and logged.
An event fires at most 20 times a second (the rest is dropped and logged), and at most 50 hook
commands run in one pass of the server (the others wait for the next), so a hook that changes what
it listens to cannot make the server spin.
A command that fails is logged and the hook is kept.
A hook runs after the change that caused it, never inside it.
.SS Notifications
To be told when an agent is blocked or done in a pane you are not looking at, run a script from
.BR agent\-state\-changed :
.PP
.nf
.RS
set\-hook \-g agent\-state\-changed "run\-shell \-b '/path/to/notify\-agent.sh'"
.RE
.fi
.PP
The script
.I examples/notify\-agent.sh
of the source tree is one that works.
The hook command is only the name of the script: the state and the pane arrive in the environment
.RB ( $IMUX_HOOK_STATE ,
.BR $IMUX_HOOK_PANE ),
because
.B #{hook_message}
and the names are written by programs in panes and so are refused in the text of a command (use
.BI #{q: name }
only where the shell reading it is meant to see data).
The script asks the server, through the
.B tmux
name every
.B run\-shell
job has while
.B tmux\-compat
is on, whether the pane is looked at
.RB ( #{window_active} ,
.BR #{pane_active} ,
.BR #{session_attached} ),
and shows the notification with
.B terminal\-notifier
or
.B osascript
with the text as arguments, never inside the code.
.B IMUX_NOTIFY_CMD
names a program to run instead; it gets the title and the text as its two arguments.
.SH EVENTS
.B imux events
keeps a connection open and prints one line of JSON for each event, as it happens, until it is
stopped or the server ends.
It is for scripts and other programs that would otherwise poll; the events are those of
.BR HOOKS ,
with the same names and variables.
.PP
.nf
{"event":"window\-renamed","seq":12,"time":1731160000000,"session":"$1","window":"@3","window_name":"build"}
.fi
.PP
.B event
is the name,
.B seq
counts up from 1 over the life of the server (a gap means the reader was not given everything),
.B time
is milliseconds since 1970, and the rest are the variables of the event without the prefix
.B hook_
(so
.B #{hook_window_name}
is
.BR window_name ),
all strings.
The values have no control characters and at most 256 characters, but a program in a pane can
choose them (names, titles, agent messages): a reader must treat them as text, never as a command.
.PP
The first line is
.B {"event":"subscribed","api":1,"seq":N}
so that a reader knows when it is connected.
.B \-\-types
names the events wanted, separated by commas (and may be repeated); an unknown name is refused.
Without it, all events but the
.BI after\- command
ones are sent; those are sent only when asked for by name.
.PP
.B \-\-wait
.I event
prints the first event of that name and exits with status 0, nothing else.
With
.BI \-\-match " key=value"
(repeatable) the event must also have those fields, and with
.BI \-\-timeout " ms"
no such event in that time is an error (status 1).
It cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-types .
.PP
Waiting for what a command caused has no race when the number is noted first.
The
.B \-\-json
result of
.BR "agent prompt" ,
.BR "agent name" ,
.B "agent start"
and
.B "pane run"
ends with
.BR seq ,
the number of the last event before the change (the socket command
.B api\-seq
prints it), and
.BI \-\-since " seq"
first sends the events kept after that number: the last 512, those of the types asked for.
Once anything has asked for a number (a subscription,
.BR api\-seq ,
such a
.B \-\-json
result), the server counts and keeps every event but the
.BI after\- command
ones, whether anyone is connected or not; before that it counts none.
If some of the events after the number are gone, or the number is from another server's life, the
reader gets
.B {"event":"events_lost"}
and
.B imux events
exits with status 1: read the state again.
.PP
Events say that something changed; they are not a log.
A reader that does not keep up (more than 64 KiB, about 500 events, waiting) is sent
.B {"event":"events_lost"}
and disconnected, and
.B imux events
exits with status 1: read the state again (for example
.BR "agent list" ,
.BR list\-windows )
and subscribe again.
.PP
.B imux api schema
prints the fields and error codes of the JSON output.
.B imux api version
prints
.BR api=1 ,
the version of this interface (the events, their names and variables, and the
.B agent
commands).
Additions keep the number; a change that could break a script raises it.
.SH PLUGINS
A plugin is a program that imux starts with the server, keeps running, and lets use the same
interface as scripts: the events (see
.BR EVENTS ),
the
.B agent
commands and
.BI "imux " command
(see
.BR COMMANDS ),
with the rights its manifest asks for.
A plugin is a directory the user put there; there is no download and no install from an address.
The source tree holds three working examples under
\fIexamples/plugins/\fP: a notification when an agent is blocked, window names from worktrees, and a
status segment with the git branch.
.PP
The directory holds a manifest, \fIplugin.conf\fP, written like the configuration file, and the
program:
.PP
.nf
name notify\-blocked
version 1
api 1
exec ./run.sh
events agent\-state\-changed
permissions read\-panes
restart on\-failure
.fi
.TP
.B name
Letters, digits, and the characters . _ \- (at most 32); the name of the link and of the files.
.TP
.B api
The version of the interface (see
.BR "imux api version" ).
A plugin for another version is refused.
.TP
.B exec
The program, a path inside the directory (not absolute, no
.BR .. ),
and its arguments.
It runs in the plugin directory.
.TP
.B events
The events it may follow with
.BR "imux events" ;
without them it may not follow any.
.TP
.B permissions
What else it may do (below).
Without them it may follow its events, run commands that only look
.RB ( list\-windows ,
.BR display\-message ,
.BR has\-session ,
.BR wait\-for ,
\&...), and use
.B "agent list"
and
.BR "agent wait" .
.TP
.B restart
.B on\-failure
(default): start it again when it ends with a failure; a clean exit is left alone.
.B never
leaves it.
.PP
Permissions:
.TP
.B read\-panes
.BR capture\-pane ,
.B "agent get"
and
.BR "pane wait\-output" .
.TP
.B send\-keys
.B send\-keys
and
.BR "pane run" .
.TP
.B set\-options
.BR set\-option ,
.BR set\-window\-option ,
.BR bind\-key ,
.BR unbind\-key ,
.BR set\-hook ,
.BR source\-file ,
.BR set\-environment .
A hook or a binding runs commands, so this reaches further than it sounds.
.TP
.B agent\-control
.BR "agent prompt" ;
the option
.B agent\-control
has to be on too.
.TP
.B windows
Every other command on sessions, windows, and panes: create, close, rename, select, respawn,
join, and start programs in them.
.PP
A plugin never gets
.BR run\-shell ,
.BR if\-shell ,
.BR pipe\-pane ,
attaching, typing as a client,
.BR kill\-server ,
saving and restoring, worktrees, or reporting as a pane; a refused request fails with a message
that names the permission.
The permissions keep a plugin from using the socket for more than it needs, so that a mistake in
a script cannot type into every pane.
They are not a sandbox: a linked plugin is a program the user chose to run, with the user's rights,
and one that means harm can do what the user's account can do, including leaving the process
tree that lets imux recognize it.
.PP
The server runs the program in a process group of its own, with no standard input, its output
appended to \fIplugins/NAME.log\fP in the state directory (cut at 1 MiB, one older generation kept
as \fI.log.1\fP) and an environment of
.BR PATH ,
.BR HOME ,
.BR USER ,
.BR LOGNAME ,
.BR SHELL ,
.BR LANG ,
.BR LC_ALL ,
.BR LC_CTYPE ,
.BR TMPDIR ,
.BR TERM ,
.B IMUX_PLUGIN
(its name),
.B IMUX_SOCKET_PATH
and
.B IMUX_EXE
(the imux program, whose directory is first in
.BR PATH ).
Nothing else of the server's environment is passed on.
The program and \fIplugin.conf\fP must belong to the user and not be writable by others.
.PP
A plugin that ends with a failure is started again after one second, then two, four and so on to a
minute; after five restarts within ten minutes it is stopped for good, the reason is logged and
shown by
.BR "plugin list" ,
and it stays off until enabled again.
Stopping a plugin (disable, unlink, the server ending) sends SIGTERM to its process group and, two
seconds later, SIGKILL.
.PP
Commands:
.TP
.BI "imux plugin link " dir
Check the manifest and the files and link the directory into the plugins directory.
It does not run until enabled.
.TP
.BI "imux plugin unlink " name
Stop it and remove the link; the directory itself is not touched.
.TP
.BI "imux plugin enable " name
.TQ
.BI "imux plugin disable " name
Switch it on or off, now (when the server runs) and from then on.
Enabled plugins start with the server.
.TP
.B "imux plugin list"
Name, version, state (enabled, disabled, or why it was stopped) and permissions.
.TP
.BI "imux plugin logs " name
The last 100 lines it printed.
.SH STATUS LINE
The status line occupies the bottom of the screen.
From the left it shows the session name in brackets, a tab for each window (number, a colon, the
name, a star after the current one) and, at the right, the time and the date.
The default is a plain green bar that needs no particular font or palette; a themed example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
set \-g status\-style "bg=#181825,fg=#cdd6f4"
set \-g status\-left "#[bg=#89b4fa,fg=#000000,bold] #S "
set \-g window\-status\-format "#[bg=#1e1e2e,fg=#a6adc8] #I  #W "
set \-g window\-status\-current\-format "#[bg=#89b4fa,fg=#000000,bold] #I  #W "
set \-g window\-status\-separator ""
.fi
.RE
.PP
The right side is laid out first and is never displaced; one wider than
.B status\-right\-length
or the terminal is cut off at its end.
If the tabs do not fit on one row the bar grows up to five rows, the extra tabs aligned under the
first and ending where the first row's tabs end; the panes are resized whenever the row count
changes.
Tabs that fit in no row are omitted, and the last row ends with an ellipsis.
.PP
With
.B "set set\-titles on"
each client's terminal is given a title (the window title, set with an operating system command)
made from
.B set\-titles\-string
(default
.BR "#S:#I:#W \- \(dq#T\(dq" ),
first when it attaches and then whenever the text changes.
The string is a format; it knows
.BR #S ,
.BR #I ,
.BR #W ,
.BR #T ,
.BR #H ,
.B #h
(and the long names such as
.BR #{session_name} )
for the current window and its active pane, and the time conversions.
Control and bidirectional characters are dropped, and a title is cut at 256 characters.
The option is off by default, and nothing is sent while it is off.
.PP
Options:
.BR status\-left ,
.BR status\-right ,
.B window\-status\-format
and
.B window\-status\-current\-format
(contents),
.B window\-status\-separator
(between tabs),
.BR window\-status\-activity\-style " and " window\-status\-bell\-style
(the look of a tab with that flag, default
.BR reverse ),
.B status\-left\-length
and
.B status\-right\-length
(lengths),
.B status\-style
(colors of the bar),
.BR status\-bg " and " status\-fg
(setting one changes that color of
.BR status\-style ),
.BR status\-left\-style " and " status\-right\-style
(added to the bar's style for those segments),
.B window\-status\-style
(every tab),
.BR window\-status\-current\-style " and " window\-status\-last\-style
(added for the current window and for the window that was current before it; an activity or bell
style comes after them, and the tab's own format after that),
.B status\-justify
(\fBleft\fP, \fBcenter\fP, \fBright\fP or \fBabsolute\-center\fP: where the tabs sit between the left
and the right segment; \fBcentre\fP and \fBabsolute\-centre\fP also work; tabs that do not all fit on the first row stay at the left),
.BR status\-format[0] " to " status\-format[4]
(a whole row drawn from its own text, left to right and cut at the edge, instead of the standard
layout).
In that text \fB#[align=left]\fP, \fB#[align=center]\fP and \fB#[align=right]\fP start the left, the
middle and the right part of the row: text before any of them is the left part, a style carries over
from one part to the next, and a part never covers the one before it.
The bar has at least one row more than the highest index set, and
.B set \-u
on the option takes the row back; tabs on a replaced row cannot be clicked, and a replaced first row
has no right segment.
.PP
These options are format strings in which the following are replaced:
.PP
.PD 0
.TP 24
