.PD
.PP
A name that is none of these is read as an option (so
.B #{status}
works), then as a user option, then as a variable of the environment new panes get
.RB ( set\-environment ,
else the server's own):
.BR #{TERM_PROGRAM} .
This holds everywhere a format is read for a pane: the format of
.BR display\-message ,
also when it is bound to a key, the titles and items of
.BR display\-menu ,
and the title and position of
.BR display\-popup .
Names in the status line also reach the variables of the pane of each window
.RB ( #{b:pane_current_path}
in a tab shows that window's pane).
.PP
Operands of these forms are expanded first and may hold further
.BR #{...} ,
nested up to eight deep.
Inside an operand a comma is written
.B #,
and a closing brace
.BR #} ;
a
.B #[style]
may hold commas as it is.
A form with the wrong number of operands is left as written.
.BI #{q: name }
is the value as one word for a shell (in single quotes); see
.BR run\-shell .
Examples:
.PP
.nf
set status\-right "#{?#{==:#{agent_state},blocked},#{agent_glyph} blocked,}"
set window\-status\-format "#{?window_active,#[bold]#W,#W}"
.fi
.PP
.PD 0
.TP 24
.BR %I:%M\ %p ,\  ...
.BR strftime (3)
conversions
.TP
.B ##
a literal
.B #
.TP
.BI #[ style ]
colors and attributes of the text after it;
.B #[default]
returns to
.B status\-style
.PD
.PP
Messages and the command prompt are drawn in
.B message\-style
and
.B message\-command\-style
when set.
.B status
and
.B status\-position
are accepted and ignored: the bar is always on, at the bottom.
.SS Commands in the status line
.BI #( command )
in any of the five format options above is replaced by the first line the command printed the
last time it ran.
The bar never waits for a command: until the first run has ended, the place stays empty.
.PP
.nf
set \-g status\-right "#(git \-C #{q:pane_current_path} rev\-parse \-\-abbrev\-ref HEAD 2>/dev/null) %H:%M"
.fi
.PP
The command is run by
.B sh \-c
as
.B run\-shell
runs a command: in a process group of its own, in the directory of the current pane of the
session, with the
.B IMUX_*
variables of that pane, with its standard input closed and its standard error thrown away.
Its text is a format, expanded before it runs, so the variables of the current pane
.RB ( #{pane_current_path} ,
.BR #{pane_id} ,
\&...) can be used in a status format; the time conversions are filled in first, so a
.B %
in a command is written
.BR %% .
Parentheses inside the command must pair up.
A variable that a program in the pane could have set (names, titles, directories) is refused
in the text of a command unless it is written
.BI #{q: name }
(see
.BR run\-shell ):
the command is not run, and the bar shows
.B [#{q:name}?]
in its place.
.PP
A command runs again when its output is older than
.B status\-interval
seconds, for as long as a bar that shows it is drawn for a client;
nothing runs for a bar nobody looks at, and a command that is no longer in any format is
forgotten after a minute.
One that runs longer than
.B status\-command\-timeout
seconds is stopped with its children and shows nothing (the reason is in the log).
At most four commands run at once and at most
.B status\-commands\-max
different ones are kept; the others show nothing.
.PP
What a command prints is only text: the first line, without control characters, at most 200
bytes, and a
.B #
in it is a
.B #
and never starts a variable, a style or another command.
.SH TERMINAL SUPPORT
.B imux
is developed against iTerm2 and Terminal.app.
It uses the alternate screen, SGR mouse reporting and synchronized output (mode 2026, ignored by
terminals without it).
.PP
Each client tells the server what its terminal can do when it attaches.
Colors go out as 24-bit when
.B TERM_PROGRAM
is
.B iTerm.app
(or
.B LC_TERMINAL
is
.BR iTerm2 ,
which survives ssh) or
.B COLORTERM
is
.B truecolor
or
.BR 24bit ;
otherwise they are mapped to the 256-color palette.
.SS Images
Programs show images with:
.IP \(bu 2
the iTerm2 inline image protocol
.RB ( "OSC 1337 ; File=" ,
also in parts with
.BR MultipartFile= ,
.B FilePart=
and
.BR FileEnd ,
as current
.B imgcat
sends, and inside a passthrough envelope, as
.B imgcat
does behind a multiplexer).
The file may be PNG, JPEG, or GIF, or a Sixel stream (what
.B imgcat
sends when given a
.B .six
file);
.IP \(bu 2
the Kitty graphics protocol
.RB ( "APC G" ;
PNG or raw pixels, direct or in chunks, optionally zlib\-compressed with
.BR o=z ,
cursor left in place with
.BR C=1 ,
pictures removed with
.B a=d
.RB ( d=a
all,
.B d=i
by id and placement id
.RB ( p= ),
.B d=n
by image number
.RB ( I= ),
.B d=c
at the cursor,
.B d=p
at a cell,
.B d=q
at a cell and
.BR z= ,
.B d=r
by a range of ids,
.B d=x
a column,
.B d=y
a row,
.B d=z
a stacking order and
.B d=f
the frames; the upper\-case forms also forget a stored picture), several placements of one picture
told apart by
.BR p= ,
pictures named by
.BR I= ,
unicode placeholders
.RB ( U=1 :
the program prints U+10EEEE with row and column diacritics, the color of the text being the
image id, and the picture shows where they are), animation
.RB ( a=f
frames,
.B a=a
to play, stop, and loop,
.B a=c
to compose; imux plays the frames itself, at least 20 milliseconds apart, at most 64 frames and
32 MB for a picture and 8 animated pictures per pane), and, only when
.B kitty\-media
allows it, pictures read from a file
.RB ( t=f ),
a temporary file that is deleted afterwards
.RB ( t=t ,
only in a temporary directory and with
.B tty\-graphics\-protocol
in its name) or shared memory
.RB ( t=s ),
so
.B kitty +kitten icat
works);
.IP \(bu 2
Sixel.
.PP
Whatever the program uses, the picture reaches the outer terminal in the way that terminal
understands.
Width and height may be given in cells, pixels, percent, or left to the picture.
.PP
A picture is decoded once and stored in the pane; the cells it covers refer to parts of it, so it
scrolls, is erased and falls out of the history exactly as text does.
The cursor ends in the first column of the next line.
Limits: 64 MB of encoded data, 32 megapixels, 16384 pixels per side (the header is checked before
decoding), 256 MB of decoded pictures per pane and 1 GB over all panes; past that the pane's oldest
pictures are dropped and their cells show a shaded block.
.PP
Pictures are decoded on a separate thread, one at a time, so a program that prints many
expensive ones cannot stop the server from answering its clients or the other panes.
Only that pane waits: what the program prints after a picture is shown after it, in order, as soon
as the picture is decoded.
A picture inside a passthrough envelope, or one that arrives when more than 256 MB of pictures are
already waiting for the decoder, is decoded in the server's own thread instead.
.PP
How a picture reaches the screen depends on the client's terminal:
.TP
.B iterm2
Each rectangle of cells showing one picture is cut out, scaled to the exact pixel size of those
cells and sent as an inline image; nothing is sent again until the rectangle changes.
Text, borders, menus, and the status line over part of a picture take precedence.
.TP
.B kitty
The same rectangles are sent as placements over the text.
Each rectangle's pixels are sent once and stored by the terminal under an id, so placing it again
costs a few bytes.
Text written over a placement does not replace it, so when any picture cell changes, all placements
are deleted and the visible ones placed again.
Everything stored is deleted when the client exits.
.TP
.B sixel
The same rectangles as Sixel, which the terminal stamps into the cells like text.
Each gets its own palette of at most 256 colors: exact if it has that few, else median cut.
.TP
.B blocks
Anywhere else: block characters, by default quadrants (two samples across and two down per cell),
each with a foreground and a background color; see
.BR image\-glyphs .
Works on any terminal, also over ssh, but coarse; it suits screenshots and diagrams more than fine
detail.
.PP
.B image\-mode
selects the way.
Any value other than
.B auto
is tried first, and when the terminal does not accept it the usual order below follows, leaving out
every way the terminal does not accept (what it accepts is known from its name, its answers to the
probe below and the environment; a terminal that does not answer the probe is trusted with the
forced way).
With
.B auto
(the default) the first that applies wins:
.B iterm2
(iTerm2, WezTerm),
.B kitty
(kitty, Ghostty),
.B sixel
(foot, mlterm, contour, or a
.B TERM
naming sixel), else
.BR blocks .
.PP
The client also asks the terminal on attach: its name (XTVERSION), whether it answers a Kitty
graphics query, and whether its device attributes list Sixel (4).
It waits at most
.B image\-probe\-timeout
milliseconds (default 600; raise it for a slow ssh link, 0 does not ask); a terminal that does not
answer is judged by its environment alone.
With
.B image\-probe auto
(the default), Terminal.app and a
.B TERM
of
.B dumb
are not asked, since they could only answer with nothing better;
.B always
asks every terminal and
.B never
none.
The client reads that option itself from the plain
.B set
lines of the configuration file, before it connects;
.B source\-file
is not followed for it.
The answer can only improve on the environment's guess, never lower it, so a terminal behind ssh or
.BR sudo ,
where the variables are lost, is still found.
Keys typed meanwhile are not lost.
.PP
Programs in a pane are told they can show pictures: the device attributes reply lists Sixel (4),
and the graphics attribute query
.RB ( "CSI ? Pi ; Pa ; Pv S" )
answers 256 color registers and a largest picture of the pane's size in pixels, so tools such as
.BR chafa ,
.B lsix
and
.B timg
choose Sixel by themselves.
This holds whatever the outer terminal is; the picture is converted for it as described above.
.PP
Panes also understand the Program Status Protocol
.RB ( "OSC 7501" ,
revision 0.4): the query
.B "OSC 7501 ; ?"
is answered with the supported states
.RB ( "idle working done blocked error" )
and kinds
.RB ( "permission question auth" ),
which is what makes an agent report what it is doing.
A pane keeps its program's last report (state, application, message) until the program exits or
the shell shows its next prompt
.RB ( "OSC 133 ; A" ),
except that
.B done
and
.B error
stay.
A report that is too long or has bad base64, control characters, an unknown state or a malformed
id is ignored as a whole.
.PP
When a pane becomes narrower than a picture, the part that no longer fits is cut off, not scaled
or wrapped, and returns when the pane is wide enough again unless something was written on that
line meanwhile.
.SS Limits of images and text
.IP \(bu 2
A picture keeps its size in cells for as long as it exists; it is clipped, never scaled, to follow
the pane.
.IP \(bu 2
Images and scrollback live in the server's memory: they are not kept across a server restart
(see
.BR PERSISTENCE ).
.IP \(bu 2
Animations are played by the server and sent to a Kitty terminal frame by frame, as new pictures,
so a large or fast one uses more bandwidth than in a Kitty terminal on its own, which matters over
a slow link.
Kitty graphics with a source rectangle or offsets inside a cell
.RB ( x= ,
.BR y= ,
.BR w= ,
.BR h= ,
.BR X= ,
.B Y=
on a placement) are accepted and the whole picture is shown.
.IP \(bu 2
The outer terminal is asked what it supports and, failing an answer, recognized from environment
variables; one that does neither (or answers wrongly) may need
.B image\-mode
set by hand.
The query goes to every terminal except Terminal.app, and one that prints unknown escape strings
shows them briefly on the alternate screen before imux clears it (set
.B image\-probe never
for it).
.IP \(bu 2
An OSC string from a program (a title, a clipboard request) is cut at 2 MiB: the rest of that
string is discarded.
Text that a program supplies for imux to show elsewhere (agent messages, process names, the output
of a
.B #()
command, hook, and event values) loses control characters, including the 8\-bit ones, and the
bidirectional formatting characters that reorder the text around them.
Pane titles and the variables
.BR #{pane_title} ,
.B #{pane_current_path}
and
.B #{pane_current_command}
are cleaned the same way when they are read.
.IP \(bu 2
What a pane program can make the server do is bounded.
A program that stops reading its own input (it sets raw mode and never reads) cannot hold up the
others: a write to it waits at most 100 ms, and for two seconds after that whatever was meant for
it (answers to its queries, a paste, focus reports) is dropped.
A picture covers at most 32768 cells, however small it is.
A Sixel picture is refused as soon as it would pass 32 million pixels or needs more than eight
times that much painting.
Pictures stored with a Kitty
.B a=t
are limited to 16 and 64 MiB together.
The answers to a program that does not read are kept up to 64 KiB; the repeat sequence
.B CSI b
repeats at most one screenful; a read turn reports at most one bell.
Kitty graphics answers carry printable ASCII only, and never a path or a size, because the answer is
typed into the pane's input.
.IP \(bu 2
A client may announce a terminal of at most 1024 columns and 1024 rows.
.B source\-file
and
.B load\-buffer
read regular files only (a pipe or a device is refused), and
.B source\-file
at most 4 MiB.
A snapshot with more than 1024 panes in all is refused.
.IP \(bu 2
A cell keeps at most three zero\-width characters (combining accents, variation selectors, joiners)
after its base character, eleven with
.BR "emoji\-width cluster" .
How wide a joined emoji is depends on the terminal, which imux cannot ask, so the right
.B emoji\-width
is a setting.
.IP \(bu 2
DCS sequences other than Sixel are ignored, except two requests that get a fixed reply:
DECRQSS for the scroll region, the cursor style and the text attributes, and XTGETTCAP for
.BR TN ,
.BR Co ,
.B colors
and
.BR RGB .
.SH CONFIGURATION
When the server starts it reads
.IR $IMUX_CONFIG ,
or else
.IR ~/.imux.conf ,
or else
.IR ~/.config/imux/imux.conf .
Syntax: one command per line, commands joined with
.BR \e; ,
.B #
comments, single and double quotes, a leading
.BR ~ ,
and
.IB name = value
lines defining variables that later lines use as
.BI ${ name }
or
.BI $ name .
A line ending in a backslash continues on the next.
A problem on a line is logged with its file name and line number, and the rest of the file is still
read.
.PP
A lone
.B {
starts a command block that ends at the matching lone
.BR } .
Inside a block, commands are separated by newlines,
.BR ; ,
or
.BR \e; ;
a block may span lines, hold
.B #
comments, and contain blocks (up to 8 deep, at most 64 KiB).
Quotes inside it are kept as written, so braces and semicolons in quotes do not end anything.
The block is the one argument of a command that takes a command, and it is read as a command line:
.B bind x { new\-window; split\-window \-h }
is
.BR "bind x new\-window \e; split\-window \-h" ,
and a block can stand for the commands of
.B if\-shell
and
.BR set\-hook ,
or be typed at the command prompt.
A command given to
.B bind
as one quoted string is read the same way.
A brace that does not stand alone (\fB#{pane_id}\fP,
.BR ${name} ,
.BR a{b} )
is text.
A lone brace in the place of the key of
.B bind
or
.B unbind
(\fBunbind {\fP, \fBbind } swap\-pane \-D\fP) is that key and not a block brace; inside a block it
is always a brace.
In a configuration file
.B bind
and
.B unbind
also take the value of
.B \-T
written against it, as in
.BR "unbind \-Tcopy\-mode\-vi x" .
In a
.B bind
block, variables are put in when the key is bound; elsewhere they are put in when the commands run.
A line number in an error is that of the line the block starts on.
.PP
Lines may depend on a condition:
.BI %if " format"
runs the lines up to the next
.BI %elif " format"
(tried only when no earlier branch ran),
.B %else
or
.B %endif
when the format expands to something that is neither empty nor
.BR 0 ;
quotes around it are dropped.
The format sees no pane: it has the names of the format list in
.B STATUS LINE
that need none
.RB ( #{version} ,
.BR #{pid} ,
.BR #{host} ,
.BR #{socket_path} ),
options, user options and environment variables, as they are when the line is read.
Conditions nest up to 16 deep, and a file has to close each one.
.BI %hidden " name=value"
defines a variable of the file like the line
.IB name = value
does.
.PP
The commands are
.B set
.RB [ \-agu ]
.IR option\ value ,
.B setw
.RB [ \-u ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR window ]
.IR option\ [value] ,
.B bind
.RB [ \-nr ]
.RB [ \-T
.IR table ]
.IR key\ command ,
.B unbind
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ \-T
.IR table ]
.IR key ,
.B source\-file
.RB [ \-nqv ]
.IR path ,
.BR display\-message ,
.BR show\-options ,
and the commands of the command prompt, which can also be typed there.
.B source\-file
reads the file named by
.IR path ;
its last part may hold
.B *
and
.B ?
(\fBsource\-file ~/.imux.d/*.conf\fP), and the files that match are read in name order (hidden files
only for a pattern that starts with a dot, at most 64).
.B \-q
makes a missing file, or a pattern with no match, no error,
.B \-n
reads and checks the files and changes nothing (the errors are named by file and line), and
.B \-v
logs each line as it is read.
The key tables are
.BR prefix ,
.BR root ,
.B copy\-mode\-vi
and
.B copy\-mode
(the emacs keys).
A copy mode binding is
.BI "bind \-T copy\-mode\-vi " "key " "send\-keys \-X " action
(see
.BR "COPY MODE" );
.B unbind \-T
removes a key, and a mode already on keeps the keys it started with.
An action imux does not have is logged and the key left as it was.
.PP
.B set
also takes
.B \-q
(an unknown option is no error) and
.B \-o
(an option that has been set keeps its value); the scope flags
.BR \-s ,
.B \-w
and
.B \-p
are accepted and mean the same as
.BR \-g ,
since there is one set of options.
.B command\-alias
is not an option but a list of other names for commands:
.B "set \-g command\-alias"
.I name=command
(or
.BI command\-alias[ n ]
to give the entry a place;
.B set \-u
with a slot removes it, without one removes all) lets
.I name
stand for the command and its arguments at the prompt, in the configuration and in
.BR "imux name" .
An alias cannot take the name of a command, is looked up once, and must expand to one command.
.PP
Options other than those below are an error.
Styles are comma separated lists of
.BI fg= color\fR,
.BI bg= color
and attributes
.RB ( bold ,
.BR dim ,
.BR italics ,
.BR underscore ,
.BR blink ,
.BR reverse ,
.BR hidden ,
.BR strikethrough ,
.BR none ),
a color being a name
.RB ( black ,
.BR red ,
.BR green ,
.BR yellow ,
.BR blue ,
.BR magenta ,
.BR cyan ,
.BR white ,
each also as
.B brightred
and so on),
.BI color N
(0 to 255; tmux's
.BI colour N
also works),
.BI # rrggbb
or
.BR default ,
for example
.BR fg=#89b4fa,bg=black,bold .
Option names with \fBcolor\fP in them, such as
.BR clock\-mode\-color ,
also work spelled \fBcolour\fP, as in tmux.
.SS General
.TP 24
.B prefix
the prefix key (default
.BR C\-b )
.TP
.B prefix2
a second prefix key, or
.B None
(the default)
.TP
.BR escape\-time ,\  repeat\-time
key timing, in milliseconds
.TP
.B double\-click\-time
the most time between presses of one button on one cell that still make a second or third press
(400 milliseconds)
.TP
.B word\-separators
the characters that end a word for the word motions of copy mode and for a double click.
With a value, those characters are punctuation and every other character that is not a blank is
part of a word (so a single blank makes a word run to the next blank).
Empty, the default, keeps the built-in rule: letters, digits and
.B _
make a word and other characters are punctuation.
The capital-letter motions only stop at blanks
.TP
.B history\-limit
lines of scrollback per pane
.TP
.BR base\-index ,\  pane\-base\-index
first window and pane number (0)
.TP
.B mouse
when off, the mouse is left to the terminal and to programs that ask for it
.TP
.B synchronize\-panes
when on, what is typed into a pane (keys, pastes and
.BR send\-keys )
also goes to every other pane of its window that is running and not in copy mode.
Default off; a window can have its own value (see
.BR set\-window\-option )
.TP
.B monitor\-activity
when on, a window that prints while nobody views it gets the flag
.B #
in
.B #{window_flags}
and its tab is drawn in
.BR window\-status\-activity\-style .
A window is viewed when it is the current window of a session with an attached client, so with no
client attached every window counts as not viewed.
The flag goes when the window is viewed or the monitor is switched off.
Default off; a window can have its own value (see
.BR set\-window\-option ).
Windows are not saved with these flags or values
.TP
.B monitor\-bell
the same for a bell, with the flag
.B !
and
.BR window\-status\-bell\-style ;
a bell wins over activity.
Default on; a window can have its own value
.TP
.B monitor\-silence
seconds of no output after which a window nobody views gets the flag
.B ~
in
.B #{window_flags}
(and
.BR #{window_silence_flag} ).
Printing takes the flag off, and so does viewing the window or setting this to 0, the default;
viewing counts as output for the time that follows.
A value set with
.B set\-window\-option
without
.B \-g
is the global one
.TP
.B silence\-action
which windows get the flag:
.BR any ,
.BR none ,
.B current
(the current window of a session with no client attached) or
.B other
(the default).
.B visual\-silence
shows the message
.B Silence in window N
to the clients of that session when the flag goes up (default off)
.TP
.B bell\-action
which bells reach the terminals of the clients:
.B any
(every bell, the default),
.BR none ,
.B current
(only bells in the current window of a session) or
.BR other .
The flag of
.B monitor\-bell
is set whatever this says
.TP
.B visual\-bell
when on, a bell that
.B bell\-action
lets through shows the message
.B Bell in window N
to the clients of that session instead of ringing.
Default off
.TP
.B visual\-activity
when on, the first output of a window nobody views that
.B monitor\-activity
flags shows the message
.B Activity in window N
to the clients of that session.
Default off
.TP
.B status
.B on
or
.BR off ;
with
.B off
there is no status line and the panes get its rows.
The numbers 2 to 5 are accepted and mean
.BR on :
the bar takes as many rows as its tabs need.
While the bar is off, a message and the command prompt are drawn over the last row of the screen
for as long as they last
.TP
.B status\-position
.B bottom
(default) or
.BR top :
the side of the screen the status line is on.
The panes, the agent dock, the pickers and the click on a tab follow it; a message and the command
prompt take the place of the bar's first row
.TP
.B mode\-keys
.B emacs
or
.BR vi ;
unless set, vi when
.B $VISUAL
(else
.BR $EDITOR )
has "vi" in its file name, else emacs
.TP
.BR display\-time ,\  display\-panes\-time ,\  status\-interval
durations
.TP
.B run\-shell\-timeout
seconds a
.B run\-shell
or
.B if\-shell
command may run (default 10)
.TP
.B status\-command\-timeout
seconds a
.BI #( command )
of the status line may run (default 2, 1 to 60)
.TP
.B status\-commands\-max
how many different
.BI #( command )
the status line keeps (default 8, 1 to 32)
.TP
.B automatic\-rename
.TQ
.B automatic\-rename\-format
window naming; the format understands
.B #{pane_current_command}
and
.B #{s/from/to/:pane_current_command}
(the text
.I from
is replaced by
.IR to ).
Several substitutions can be chained, separated by semicolons; they are applied from left to
right, each to the result of the one before:
.B #{s/2.1.325/claude/;s/aca\-claude\-go/claude/:pane_current_command}
.TP
.B default\-shell
the shell started in panes; empty means
.BR $SHELL ,
and
.B /bin/sh
when that is unset
.TP
.B shell\-syntax
how commands typed into panes are quoted and chained:
.BR auto ,
.BR posix ,
.BR fish ,
.B nu
or
.BR pwsh .
.B auto
follows the shell's name; for an unknown shell such as tcsh, imux types nothing that needs quoting
or chaining
.TP
.B remain\-on\-exit
.BR off ", " on " or " failed :
what to do with a pane whose program ended.
Off (the default) closes it.
On keeps it, showing what the program printed and the line
.IR "Pane is dead (status N)" ;
it takes no input, stays in its place until
.B kill\-pane
or
.BR respawn\-pane ,
and fires
.BR pane\-died .
.B failed
keeps only the panes whose program did not end with status 0.
A session whose last pane is a dead one stays alive
.TP
.B focus\-events
on (default) or off: ask the terminal to say when its window gains or loses the keyboard focus.
The reports fire
.B client\-focus\-in
and
.BR client\-focus\-out ,
and go on to the active pane when its program asked for them (editors use them to reread files).
A pane that becomes the active pane of its window is told the same way, and fires
.BR pane\-focus\-in " and " pane\-focus\-out .
Off: no report is requested, so no program in a pane gets any
.TP
.B command\-from\-panes
what programs in panes may run: off (look only), spawned (act on the panes they spawned) or on
(see
.BR COMMANDS )
.TP
.B spawned\-depth
how many levels of panes a program in a pane may spawn below a pane you made (2, from 1 to 8)
.TP
.B spawned\-panes\-max
how many live spawned panes one pane you made may have (16, from 1 to 256)
.TP
.B pane\-min\-width
the fewest columns either pane may have after a split asked for by a program in a pane (20, from 1
to 500)
.TP
.B pane\-min\-height
the fewest rows either pane may have after such a split (5, from 1 to 200)
.TP
.B tmux\-compat
give new panes
.BR $TMUX ,
.B $TMUX_PANE
and a
.B tmux
first on
.B PATH
(on; see
.BR "Programs that look for a terminal multiplexer" )
.TP
.B agent\-control
let scripts type into the panes of agents with
.B "imux agent prompt"
and start them with
.B "imux agent start"
(off)
.TP
.B agent\-stall\-time
seconds (1 to 300, default 5) an agent may show neither
.B working
nor
.B blocked
after
.B "imux agent prompt \-\-wait"
typed its text before the call fails as
.I prompt_stalled
.TP
.B agent\-kind
space separated
.IB kind = program
entries, the agents
.B "imux agent start \-\-kind"
may run: the kind is named like an agent, the program a plain command name that is also in
.BR restore\-commands .
Empty by default (then only
.B worktree\-command
can be started)
.TP
.B update\-environment
space separated variable names (default
.BR "DISPLAY SSH_ASKPASS SSH_AUTH_SOCK SSH_AGENT_PID SSH_CONNECTION WINDOWID XAUTHORITY" ):
each time a client attaches, the ones it has are copied for new panes and the ones it lacks are
dropped, so a replaced ssh login leaves no pane with a dead
.BR SSH_AUTH_SOCK .
The client reads it from the plain
.B set
lines, like
.B image\-probe\-timeout
.TP
.B window\-size
which client decides the size of a window when several look at the session:
.B smallest
(default),
.B largest
(the others see the top left part),
.B latest
(the one that typed or was resized last), or
.B manual
(always
.BR default\-size ).
Changing it applies at once
.TP
.B default\-size
.IB cols x rows
(default
.BR 80x24 ,
10 to 1000 each): the size of a session nobody looks at yet, and of every window under
.B "window\-size manual"
.TP
.B strip\-environment
space separated variable names, or starts ending in
.BR * ,
that the server removes from the environment of new panes (default: the per-window identity of the
terminal that started the server,
.BR "ITERM_SESSION_ID ITERM_PROFILE KITTY_WINDOW_ID KITTY_PID KITTY_PUBLIC_KEY WEZTERM_PANE WEZTERM_UNIX_SOCKET ZELLIJ* STY" ,
and the session markers of an agent started in that terminal).
.B WINDOW
goes with
.BR STY .
A name that
.B set\-environment
or a
.B \-e
flag gives a value is kept, and an empty option strips nothing.
Capability hints such as
.B TERM_PROGRAM
and
.BR LC_TERMINAL ,
.BR TERM ,
and secrets such as API keys stay
.SS Appearance
.TP 24
.BR pane\-border\-style ,\  pane\-active\-border\-style ,\  mode\-style
styles
.TP
.BR display\-panes\-color ,\  display\-panes\-active\-color
the background of the number that
.B display\-panes
draws on a pane that is not the active one, and on the active one; default, the built-in colors
.TP
.BR pane\-border\-status ,\  pane\-border\-format
with
.B top
or
.B bottom
(default
.BR off )
every pane gives its first or last row to a label: the program in the pane has one row less, and the
row shows the format
.B pane\-border\-format
(default the pane index, reversed in the active pane, and the title) on the divider line, in the
style of the dividers.
A pane of one row has no label.
A click on the label row does nothing; the label is not part of mouse keys.
Terminal scrolling of full-width panes is not used while labels are on
.TP
.B pane\-border\-lines
the characters of the dividers between panes: single, double, heavy, simple (plain
.BR - ,
.B |
and
.BR + )
or spaces (default single)
.TP
.BR window\-style ,\  window\-active\-style
the colors a pane's cells take where the program did not choose one (the terminal's default):
every pane takes
.BR window\-style ,
and the active pane then takes
.B window\-active\-style
on top of it; a style set with
.B setw
without
.B \-g
is the global one (default; default)
.TP
.BR message\-style ,\  message\-command\-style
styles of messages and the command prompt
.TP
.BR menu\-style ,\  menu\-selected\-style ,\  menu\-border\-style
styles of a menu, of its chosen item and of its border (default; bg=yellow,fg=black; default)
.TP
.B menu\-border\-lines
line set of a menu's border: single, rounded, double, heavy, simple, padded, or none (single)
.TP
.BR popup\-style ,\  popup\-border\-style
styles of a popup and of its border (default; default)
.TP
.B popup\-border\-lines
line set of a popup's border, as for menus (single)
.TP
.B popup\-from\-panes
let programs in panes run
.B display\-popup
(on; see
.BR Popups )
.TP
.B status\-*
the options under
.B "STATUS LINE"
.TP
.B clock\-mode\-color
one color (blue)
.TP
.B clock\-mode\-style
24 or 12 hours (24)
.SS Clipboard and buffers
.TP 24
.B buffer\-limit
automatic paste buffers kept (50)
.TP
.B copy\-command
shell command that receives the copied text (default
.B pbcopy
on macOS; on Linux the first of
.BR wl\-copy " (under Wayland), " "xclip \-selection clipboard" " and " "xsel \-b"
that is installed, and nothing is run when there is none)
.TP
.B set\-clipboard
.B external
(default),
.B on
or
.BR off .
Text copied in copy mode goes to the terminal's clipboard unless
.BR off .
Only
.B on
lets a program in a pane set the clipboard with the OSC 52 sequence, and then only a well formed
write (a selection of letters and digits, base64 data of at most 1 MiB), only to the terminals
that show that pane, at most once a second per pane; the read form is never answered
.SS Images
.TP 24
.B image\-mode
.BR auto ,
.BR blocks ,
.BR iterm2 ,
.B kitty
or
.B sixel
(see
.BR "TERMINAL SUPPORT" )
.TP
.B image\-probe\-timeout
milliseconds, 0 to 5000 (600)
.TP
.B image\-probe
.BR auto ,
.B always
or
.B never
(see
.BR "TERMINAL SUPPORT" )
.TP
.B image\-band\-rows
0 to 64 (4).
A picture is drawn to the terminal in bands this many cell rows high, cut at rows of the picture
itself, and each band is encoded once and kept.
Scrolling a large picture (in copy mode, or as output scrolls) then reuses the bands instead of
encoding everything visible again at each step, and something laid over the picture, like the
position shown in copy mode, only redraws the band it touches.
0 draws each rectangle of the picture in one piece.
Bands apply to terminals that are sent inline pictures; see
.BR image\-prefer
.TP
.B image\-glyphs
.BR quadrant ,
.B sextant
or
.B octant
(quadrant).
The characters pictures are drawn with where the terminal shows no pictures: two samples across and
two (quadrants), three (sextants) or four (octants) down each cell.
More samples are finer but need a font that has the characters, and a terminal cannot be asked
whether it does: if the cells show boxes, use a coarser set
.TP
.B image\-dither
.B none
or
.B ordered
(none).
For the two ways a picture is cut down to a small palette: the colors of the character cells of a
terminal without 24-bit color (mapped to its 256 colors), and a Sixel picture with more than 256
colors (reduced to 256 registers).
.B ordered
adds a fixed 8 by 8 threshold pattern to each color before it is mapped, so a smooth gradient
becomes a fine pattern of the two nearest palette entries instead of bands.
The pattern is indexed by the place in the picture, never by where the picture is on the screen, so
a picture that scrolls keeps its pattern and nothing shimmers.
A client with 24-bit color, a picture of 256 colors or fewer, and the iTerm2 and Kitty ways of
drawing are not changed.
A change repaints the pictures
.TP
.B image\-scroll
on or off (on).
For terminals that are sent inline or Sixel pictures: when a pane that is as wide as the screen
scrolls, in copy mode or as output goes by, the rows already on the screen are moved with a scroll
region (the pictures in them move along) and only the rows that came into view are drawn.
Nothing is added to the terminal's own scrollback, and the scrollback of imux is not affected
.TP
.B image\-prefer
.BR auto ,
.B kitty
or
.B inline
(auto).
With
.B auto
(and
.BR inline )
pictures go by the iTerm2 protocol first, then Kitty, then Sixel, and a terminal that has none gets
color blocks (truecolor, else 256 colors).
.B kitty
shows pictures on an iTerm2 that answers the Kitty graphics query through that protocol instead:
each picture is sent once and only parts of it are placed afterwards.
Read by the client from the configuration file, like
.B image\-probe\-timeout
.TP
.B image\-kitty\-placement
.B source
or
.B crop
(source).
Terminals shown pictures through the Kitty protocol get each picture once and every redraw shows a
rectangle of it
.RB ( source );
.B crop
stores every part separately
.TP
.B image\-copy\-text
the text that stands for a picture in copy mode, in
.B capture\-pane
and in the agent preview, once for each run of picture cells ("[image]"; empty leaves pictures out)
.TP
.B kitty\-media
.B none
(default),
.B files
(Kitty
.B t=f
and
.BR t=t )
or
.B all
(also
.BR t=s ).
The server reads the file or shared memory a program names, as the user of the server.
That is meant for programs running on the same machine; a program on another machine (over ssh)
would make the server open files of its user, which is why it is off.
Files under
.BR /proc ,
.BR /sys ,
.B /dev
and anything but a regular file are refused; a shared memory object is mapped, so a program that
shrinks it meanwhile can crash the server
.TP
.B emoji\-width
.B parts
(default) or
.BR cluster :
how wide a joined emoji (a zero\-width joiner sequence, a flag, a skin tone, a character followed by
the emoji selector) is.
With
.B cluster
it is one glyph of two cells, as terminals that join them draw it; with
.B parts
each part keeps its own width, which suits terminals that do not
.SS Persistence
See
.BR PERSISTENCE .
.TP 24
.B autosave\-interval
seconds, 0 for never (300)
.TP
.B autosave\-history
how many rolling copies of the autosave to keep, 0 for none (24); see
.B PERSISTENCE
.TP
.B autosave\-history\-interval
the least seconds between two rolling copies (900)
.TP
.B restore\-on\-start
on
.TP
.B restore\-commands
program names separated by spaces (default
.BR "claude vim nvim nano less man htop ssh" )
.TP
.B restore\-claude
on
.TP
.B claude\-programs
program names separated by spaces that count as the agent, by the file name of the program or of
its first argument (default
.BR claude ).
Name a wrapper script or renamed binary here; nothing else is built in
.SS Agents
See
.B "AGENT STATUS"
and
.BR "AGENT PANEL" .
.TP 24
.BR agent\-glyph\-working ,\  agent\-glyph\-blocked ,\  agent\-glyph\-done ,\  agent\-glyph\-error
glyphs
.TP
.BR agent\-style\-working " ... " agent\-style\-error
their styles
.TP
.BR agent\-panel ,\  agent\-panel\-jump ,\  agent\-panel\-preview ,\  agent\-panel\-preview\-lines ,\  agent\-panel\-sort ,\  agent\-overlay\-width ,\  agent\-overlay\-height
the panel
.SS Worktrees
See
.BR WORKTREES .
.TP 24
.B repos\-dir
the directory
.B repo\-wizard
scans
.TP
.B worktree\-base
empty: automatic
.TP
.B worktree\-detach
off
.TP
.B worktree\-name\-pattern
a regular expression (default
.BR "[A\-Za\-z0\-9][A\-Za\-z0\-9._/\-]*" ;
an invalid one is refused by
.BR set )
.TP
.B worktree\-path
default
.BR #{repo}\-worktrees/#{name} ,
relative to the repository's parent
.TP
.B worktree\-branch\-format
default
.B #{name}
.TP
.B worktree\-layout
default
.B claude|shell
.TP
.BR worktree\-command ,\  worktree\-start ,\  worktree\-setup ,\  agent\-kind
empty
.SS User options
A name starting with
.B @
(then letters, digits,
.BR _ ,
.B \-
and
.BR . ,
up to 63 characters) is an option of your own: free text that formats and scripts read.
.B set \-g @name value
stores it,
.B \-a
appends,
.B \-u
removes it, and
.B setw
and
.B set \-w
work the same (a user option has one scope, the server's).
.B show\-options
lists them after the others and prints one with
.BR "show\-options \-gv @name" .
.B #{@name}
gives the value, or nothing when there is none, in
.BR display\-message ,
.B \-F
formats, menu and popup titles, the status line, and shell text.
At most 4096 are kept, each at most 4096 bytes, and control characters are dropped from a value.
They are not saved with a session: the configuration sets them again.
.PP
Shell text (a
.B run\-shell
command, a
.B #(command)
segment) may use
.B #{@name}
only when the value was given by the configuration, a key or the prompt.
A value given by a program in a pane (possible only with
.BR command\-from\-panes )
must be written
.BR #{q:@name} ,
as for any variable a pane could have set.
.SS Accepted and ignored
Some options that other programs' configuration files set, and that imux does not need
.RB ( default\-terminal ,
.BR terminal\-overrides ,
.BR extended\-keys ,
.BR aggressive\-resize ,
.B allow\-rename
and a few dozen more), are accepted by
.BR set ,
hold no value and are not listed by
.BR show\-options .
An array option may carry an index
.RB ( user\-keys[0] ).
.B imux check\-config
lists each one as
.BR ignored\-option .
A name that is neither an option of
.B imux
nor in this list is an error.
.PP
.B show\-options
lists the options that nothing has read yet; the server also writes that list to its log when it
exits.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.imux.conf
The configuration file.
.TP
.I ~/.config/imux/plugins/NAME
The plugins (links to their directories), and
.I ~/.local/state/imux/plugins
for what the server keeps of them: \fINAME.enabled\fP, \fINAME.crashed\fP (why it was stopped) and
\fINAME.log\fP.
See
.BR PLUGINS .
.TP
.I $TMPDIR/imux\-UID/default
The server socket.
The directory has mode 0700 and the socket 0600 from the moment it is created.
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
replaces
.B TMPDIR
on Linux when it is unset and the variable is an absolute path.
.I /tmp
is used if neither is set or the path would be too long for a socket.
.TP
.I $TMPDIR/imux\-UID/server.log
The server log, one JSON object per line, mode 0600
.RI ( server\-name.log
for a named server).
.TP
.I $TMPDIR/imux\-UID/shims/tmux
A link to the
.B imux
program, in a directory of mode 0700 owned by you, made by the server when it starts a pane with
.B tmux\-compat
on; a file or directory in its place is left alone and the pane gets no link.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/imux/autosave.imux
The autosave, with the one before it in
.IR autosave.prev ,
used if the newest file is damaged.
When the server starts and the newest file does not read back (damaged, or written by a newer
version), it is moved to
.IR autosave.bad.MILLISECONDS ,
so the first new save does not replace the good previous copy; the newest three of those are kept
and nothing restores from them.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/imux/snapshots/NAME.imux
Named snapshots.
.PP
Snapshot files are text, written to a temporary file and renamed so that a crash never leaves a
half-written one; the directory has mode 0700 and the files 0600.
A file with a version this
.B imux
does not know, or one cut short, is refused with a message in the log, and the server starts without
it.
.PP
The server serves only connections from your own user id, and at most 256 at a time.
A panic is logged with its location and a backtrace and contained: a pane whose output handling
panicked is closed and a client whose input handling panicked is disconnected, while other sessions
continue.
A client that panics resets the terminal before exiting.
.PP
The server writes no core file (it would hold what the panes showed) and raises its open file limit,
since each pane, client, and helper takes descriptors.
Programs in panes get the limits the server was started with, no descriptor of the server (signal
pipe, other panes' terminals, sockets) and the default SIGPIPE.
Git is run without
.BR GIT_DIR ,
.BR GIT_WORK_TREE ,
.B GIT_INDEX_FILE
and the other variables that point at a different repository, and never asks for a password.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
In every pane:
.B IMUX_ENV
(always 1),
.B IMUX_PANE_ID
(the pane's number) and
.B IMUX_SOCKET_PATH
(the server's socket); see
.BR "AGENT STATUS" .
With
.B tmux\-compat
on also
.BR TMUX ,
.B TMUX_PANE
and
.BR IMUX_SHIM_DIR .
.TP
.B IMUX_SOCKET_NAME
The name of the server to use (see
.BR SYNOPSIS ).
.TP
.B IMUX_ALLOW_NESTED
Set to 1 to let
.BR imux ,
.B imux new
and
.B imux attach
attach a client from inside a pane of the server they attach to.
Without it they refuse, because the client would show the screen inside itself; commands that only
talk to the server (\fBimux ls\fR, \fBimux new\-window\fR, ...) work in a pane.
A program that only inherited the variables of another server (a different
.BR TMPDIR ,
.BR \-L )
is not held back.
To start imux from a shell startup file only outside imux, test
.B IMUX_ENV
first, as in
.BR "[ \-z \(dq$IMUX_ENV\(dq ] && exec imux" .
.TP
.B IMUX_PLUGINS_DIR
The directory of the plugin links, instead of
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/imux/plugins
or
.IR ~/.config/imux/plugins .
In a plugin: \fBIMUX_PLUGIN\fP, \fBIMUX_SOCKET_PATH\fP and \fBIMUX_EXE\fP (see
.BR PLUGINS ).
.TP
.B IMUX_CONFIG
The configuration file to read instead of the default locations.
.TP
.B IMUX_STATE_DIR
The directory for snapshots instead of
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/imux
or
.IR ~/.local/state/imux .
.TP
.B SHELL
The program run in each new pane unless
.B default\-shell
is set;
.I /bin/sh
if neither is.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
The location of the socket directory (on Linux
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
when this is unset).
.TP
.B IMUX_LOG
The log level: debug, info, or error.
Any other value, or none, selects warn.
.TP
.B IMUX_LOG_FILE
A file to which the client appends its log lines.
.TP
.B IMUX_RECORD_OUTPUT
A path.
Every byte the client writes to its terminal is also appended to this path followed by a dot and the
process id of the client (mode 0600; an existing file or a link is refused).
For finding out what a terminal was sent when a picture flickers:
.B cat
the file in that terminal to see it again.
.TP
.BR TERM_PROGRAM ", " LC_TERMINAL ", " COLORTERM
Read by the client to find out what its terminal can do; see
.BR "TERMINAL SUPPORT" .
